// Generated by tools/obsidian_pack.py (do not edit): the OLYMPUS starter vault as one module, for the Mind page's Teach screen and its demo brain.
// The same notes as starter_pack.json ({path, text} each). Loaded on demand with import('./starter/starter_pack.js').
export default {"v":1,"kind":"olympus-starter-pack","sha":"2959f7453edb","notes":[{"path":"00 Home.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [home, moc]\n---\n\n# 00 Home\n\n> Your starter vault: the trading system, the prop firm's rules, what to do when, what was tried, the money, and the templates. Every note links to the others.\n\n## Start here\n1. Open the Files app (or iCloud Drive) and find the folder you unzipped.\n2. In Obsidian choose *Open folder as vault* (or copy the folder into your existing vault).\n3. Set Templates to the *Templates* folder, then open [[Zlarior LIT strategy]]. In Jarvis, connect the vault so it can read [[Me]].\n\n## The map\n- [[Trading hub]] - strategy, risk, glossary, checklists\n- [[Playbooks]] - what to do when\n- [[Research hub]] - what the research found\n- [[Money hub]] - accounts and the numbers\n- [[Templates]] - daily, trade journal, weekly, Kalshi, ideas\n- [[OLYMPUS apps]] - how to use each app\n- [[Goals]] | [[Me]] | [[How Jarvis reads this vault]]\n\n## Read these first\n- [[The bot in one minute]] - The bot waits for price to poke through an obvious high or low, trap the late traders, then turn. It buys (or sells) the turn with a stop right...\n- [[Step 1 - mark the levels]] - Before anything happens, the bot lists the obvious highs and lows: session highs and lows, yesterday, last week, last month, opening gaps, equal...\n- [[Step 6 - the stop]] - The stop goes 1 tick beyond the extreme of the sweep, never a fixed distance. Under 8 points or over 80 points means no trade.\n- [[Step 7 - the target]] - The target is the 18:00 open plus (long) or minus (short) one day's expected move from the VIX band. It must be at least 1.5R away, and profit is...\n- [[The drawdown floor]] - Your account fails if your balance, with any open trade, touches the floor. On the 50K the floor starts at $48,000.\n- [[When I have a loss]] - A stop-out is a normal cost of the system, not a mistake. Do nothing special: no revenge trade, no bigger size.\n- [[Daily routine]] - A simple day: look before the session, leave the bot alone during it, review after. Three short steps each.\n- [[Money, not only R]] - Every result shows net dollars and dollars a month next to PF (profit factor) and R. R alone hides how much real money is at stake.\n- [[The graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends]] - Many ideas were tested properly and dropped. Before you suggest a new strategy idea, search here first.\n\n## Every day\n[[Daily routine]] then [[Trading checklists]]. Write the day in a [[Daily notes]] note and each trade in the [[Trade journal]].\n"},{"path":"Daily/Daily notes.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [daily, moc]\n---\n\n# Daily notes\n\n> Put one note a day here, named with the date (for example 2026-11-04). Make it from the [[Daily note]] template.\n\nThe routine: [[Daily routine]]. Jarvis finds today's note by its date: [[How Jarvis reads this vault]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Goals.md","text":"---\ntype: goals\ntags: [goals, trading]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Goals\n\n> The targets, in the owner's words, kept short. Change them when they change.\n\n## Trading\n- Pass the MFFU Builder 50K eval within 2 weeks, never more than 3 weeks.\n- 2 to 4 trades a week from the bot.\n- Show money, not only R: net dollars and dollars a month.\n- Go live only when the owner decides, present at the desk.\n\n## The system\n- OLYMPUS and Jarvis: the home of everything.\n- Obsidian connected to everything, so Jarvis thinks like me. See [[Me]].\n\nRelated: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]], [[Money hub]], [[00 Home]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Journal/Trade journal.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [journal, moc]\n---\n\n# Trade journal\n\n> One note per trade, made from the [[Trade journal entry]] template. Fields: setup, size, plan followed, emotion, lesson.\n\nEvery Sunday read the week back with the [[Weekly review]] template. The rules you check against: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]], [[Risk rules]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Accounts plan.md","text":"---\ntype: money\ntags: [money, accounts]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/plutus/plans.json\"]\n---\n\n# Accounts plan\n\n> The accounts plan in short. Numbers are net of every eval fee, after costs, from the bot's real LIT trades and the research files (`tools/web_public/plutus/plans.json`).\n\n## What each account is\n- **LIT on the Builder 50K** (eval price $91.80): funded about $907 a month in Jan-Jun, $914 in Jul-Oct; 2-week eval pass 52.9% / 58.6%.\n- **ATLAS on a Builder 25K** (eval price $43): funded about $582 a month in Jan-Jun, $517 in Jul-Oct; 2-week eval pass 65.5% / 43.5%.\n- **BLITZ on a Builder 25K** (eval price $63): funded about $322 a month in Jan-Jun, $82 in Jul-Oct; 2-week eval pass 49.6% / 36.2%.\n- **BLITZ on a Builder 25K** (eval price $63): eval pass within 2 weeks 49.6% / 36.2%; net per month from the purchase $289 / $52.\n\n## How many accounts\n| Accounts | Jan-Jun 2026 | Jul-Oct 2026 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 account: LIT 50K | $718 a month | $477 a month |\n| 1 account: ATLAS 25K | $623 a month | $519 a month |\n| 1 account: BLITZ 25K | $191 a month | $140 a month |\n| 2 accounts: LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K | $1,341 a month | $996 a month |\n| 2 accounts: LIT 50K + BLITZ 25K | $909 a month | $616 a month |\n| 3 accounts: LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K + BLITZ 25K | $1,532 a month | $1,136 a month |\n| 4 accounts: + a LIT copy on a 25K | $1,635 a month | $1,175 a month |\n| 6 accounts: + a 2nd ATLAS 25K + a 2nd BLITZ 25K | $1,669 a month | $1,203 a month |\n\nThree accounts is the MFFU cap. $1,100-1,800 a month after eval fees: the model's Jul-Oct result up to the sum of the three single-account figures for Jan-Jun.\n\n## Notes\n- Copies of one strategy take the same trades (MFFU forbids long and short at once across your accounts), so those accounts rise and fall together: the study measured a 0.85 correlation between two of them. LIT, ATLAS and BLITZ are different strategies: their daily correlation in 2026 is LIT x ATLAS +0.14, LIT x BLITZ -0.06, ATLAS x BLITZ 0.00.\n- The study covers 6 months from the purchase. Later months continue at the months 4-6 rate: an extension, not a measurement.\n- MFFU allows 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25K per person: 3 accounts is the cap; more accounts must be another firm.\n\nRelated: [[Plutus numbers]], [[Second strategies]], [[MFFU rules that matter]], [[Money hub]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Money hub.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [money, moc]\n---\n\n# Money hub\n\n> Money, not only R: every result shows net dollars and dollars a month.\n\n- [[Accounts plan]]\n- [[Plutus numbers]]\n- [[How good is it The headline]] - 2026 so far: PF (profit factor) 4.19, +$43,820 on paper with costs in. The honest number to expect is the later half: PF 2.91, +$9,919 (about $2,670...\n- [[Money, not only R]] - Every result shows net dollars and dollars a month next to PF (profit factor) and R. R alone hides how much real money is at stake.\n- [[Eval speed - 2 weeks]] - An eval should be passed or failed within 2 weeks (10 sessions) and never take more than 3 weeks to pass.\n- [[Two halves rule]] - A rule change is only kept if it also works on the second half of the data it was not picked on.\n- [[MFFU rules that matter]]\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Numbers/Eval speed - 2 weeks.md","text":"---\ntype: numbers\ntags: [money, numbers, mffu]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\", \"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Eval speed - 2 weeks\n\n> An eval should be passed or failed within 2 weeks (10 sessions) and never take more than 3 weeks to pass.\n\nEach run shows p10 and f10 (passed or failed within 10 sessions), the share decided in 10, and the median sessions to pass.\n\nOn the Jul to Oct check, 58.6% passed and 20.9% failed within 2 weeks.\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Numbers/How good is it The headline.md","text":"---\ntype: numbers\ntags: [money, numbers, strategy]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\"]\n---\n\n# How good is it The headline\n\n> 2026 so far: [[PF (profit factor)]] 4.19, +$43,820 on paper with costs in. The honest number to expect is the later half: PF 2.91, +$9,919 (about $2,670 a month).\n\nQuote the Jul to Oct check, not the best-looking number. It was not used to pick the rules.\n\nPF 2.91 means every $1 lost was matched by $2.91 won.\n\n2021 to 2025 is only a reference line. The bot is tuned and judged on 2026.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Which result should you quote as the one to expect?\n**A:** The Jul to Oct check, PF 2.91\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Numbers/Money, not only R.md","text":"---\ntype: numbers\ntags: [money, numbers, mffu]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\", \"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# Money, not only R\n\n> Every result shows net dollars and dollars a month next to [[PF (profit factor)]] and [[R]]. R alone hides how much real money is at stake.\n\nEval prices you pay: Builder 50K $91.80, Builder 25K $63, one-time, $0 activation. Not the list price.\n\nOn funded accounts you keep 80% of each payout.\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Numbers/Two halves rule.md","text":"---\ntype: numbers\ntags: [money, numbers, strategy]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Two halves rule\n\n> A rule change is only kept if it also works on the second half of the data it was not picked on.\n\nPick on Jan to Jun 2026, check on Jul to Oct 2026, report both.\n\nThis is how the bot avoids fooling itself with luck.\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Money/Plutus numbers.md","text":"---\ntype: money\ntags: [money, plutus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/plutus/plans.json\"]\n---\n\n# Plutus numbers\n\n> The Plutus app's numbers as a note. Spans: Jan-Jun 2026, Jul-Oct 2026 (the honest check) and 2021-25 (reference only).\n\n## Plans\n| Plan | Eval price (list) | Target | Max loss | Split |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| MFFU Builder 50K | $91.80 ($153) | $3,000 | $2,000 | 80% |\n| MFFU Builder 25K | $63 ($105) | $1,500 | $1,000 | 80% |\n| Lucid LucidPro 50K | $115.40 ($192) | $3,000 | $2,000 | 90% |\n| FundedNext Rapid Daily 50K | $169.99 ($299.98) | $3,000 | $2,000 | 90% |\n\n## All the bots together (per month, net of fees)\n| Span | Per month | Months 4-6 per month | Months with a loss over 6 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | $2,655 | $3,292 | 3.1% |\n| H2 | $2,147 | $2,792 | 5.2% |\n| 2021-25 | $-92 | $100 | 83.9% |\n\nBuilt 2026-11-04 05:27. Source: research/lit/funded_firms.json (research/writeups/lit-funded-firms.md, rerun 2026-11-04 05:27 with the live presets: eval room 0.8 then safe_eval, funded 60% of the room <= $1,500 and a winner <= $2,000; 1,500 paths x 12...\n\nRelated: [[Accounts plan]], [[Money hub]], [[Money, not only R]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Money hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Arena - ranks and labours.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/arena/\"]\n---\n\n# Arena - ranks and labours\n\n> Arena gives you XP, ranks, 12 Labours, daily quests and streaks. XP comes only from things that can be checked, like the bots running.\n\nIt is a game layer over real events. Paper only.\n\nOpen it when: you want a small goal for the day.\n\nThe one thing to look at: today's quests.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Ares - the war room.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/ares/\"]\n---\n\n# Ares - the war room\n\n> Ares is the checklist and the risk calculator. You type the entry and the stop and it says GO, WAIT or HALT and how many contracts fit.\n\nSize follows the stop: a wide stop means fewer contracts.\n\nOpen it when: before you place any trade by hand.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the verdict: GO, WAIT or HALT.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Athena - this library.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/\"]\n---\n\n# Athena - this library\n\n> Athena is this library. Search it, tap a shelf, read the playbooks or take the quiz.\n\nQuiz me gives 5 questions from the library and saves nothing.\n\nOpen it when: you forget a rule or a word, or something happens and you want the playbook.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the search box: type one word.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Bridge - the ChatGPT sync steps.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"handoff/ONBOARD.md\", \"handoff/SYNC.md\"]\n---\n\n# Bridge - the ChatGPT sync steps\n\n> Paste the onboarding text into ChatGPT once. After that ChatGPT and Claude stay in step through one GitHub repo.\n\n1. One time: open handoff/ONBOARD.md in the repo and paste it into ChatGPT.\n\n2. Copy a job card from the Bridge page and give it to ChatGPT.\n\n3. ChatGPT builds it and adds the files under handoff/inbox/<job>/ in a pull request. It never edits the bots.\n\n4. Tell Claude \"Bridge: <job> done\". Claude checks it, wires it in and ships it.\n\nRules for ChatGPT: paper only, no keys or logins, no changes to the engine or live settings.\n\nOpen it when: you want ChatGPT to build something.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the job card you copy.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Bridge - working with ChatGPT.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"handoff/ONBOARD.md\", \"handoff/SYNC.md\", \"tools/web_public/bridge/\"]\n---\n\n# Bridge - working with ChatGPT\n\n> Bridge is a page of jobs you can hand to ChatGPT (art, text, bulk work). Claude does the engine, the tests and the publishing.\n\nIt never touches the bots. ChatGPT only adds files in a folder called handoff/inbox.\n\nOpen it when: you want ChatGPT to make something for OLYMPUS.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the list of job cards.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/DAPHNE - the strategy tree.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/tree/\", \"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# DAPHNE - the strategy tree\n\n> DAPHNE searches a tree of rules. Each branch adds one condition to a plain level trade. It cuts branches that cannot win and tests the best path on data it never saw.\n\nToday's card says the top rules are no better than chance, so nothing changes in the bot.\n\nOpen it when: you want to know if a rule idea holds up.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the out-of-sample result. If it is no better than chance, it is paper only.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Data City - tested ideas.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/datacity/\"]\n---\n\n# Data City - tested ideas\n\n> Data City draws every tested time of day, confluence and market state as a building. It is the machine that tests ideas against the data.\n\nBefore you suggest an idea, look at [[The graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends|the graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends]]. Many ideas died here.\n\nOpen it when: you are curious what was tested, or you have a new idea.\n\nThe one thing to look at: how many buildings pass the noise guard, and how many a placebo test lets through.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Fates - your odds.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/fates/\"]\n---\n\n# Fates - your odds\n\n> Fates runs a Monte Carlo (many simulated futures) on the bots' own results and shows how likely an eval is to pass, to fail, and what a month is worth. Paper only.\n\nIt is a simulation of the past. It is not a promise.\n\nOpen it when: you start a new eval, or wonder how risky it is.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the pass odds and the fail odds next to each other.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Hermes - your week.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/hermes/\"]\n---\n\n# Hermes - your week\n\n> Hermes (Wrapped) tells the week of the bots as stories: net dollars, dollars a month, the best and worst trade, evals and discipline.\n\nOpen it when: on Friday evening or the weekend.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the net for the week and the best trade.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis - Think for me.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis - Think for me\n\n> Tap Think for me (or say \"think for me\" or \"what would you do\") and Jarvis gives you 3 ideas of what to do next, most urgent first.\n\nWith no key it uses rules over your numbers: the 7-day rule, room left, a stale feed, due to-dos, a reminder soon, tomorrow's plan.\n\nWith a free key a model writes the 3 ideas instead. If anything fails, the rules answer.\n\nEvery idea is an ask, an open or a mode switch. It is never a trade.\n\nOpen it when: you do not know what to do next.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the first idea. It is the urgent one.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis - smart mode (free key).md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis - smart mode (free key)\n\n> Smart mode makes Jarvis answer anything, not only your numbers. It needs a free key from Groq, Gemini or OpenRouter. The setup screen walks you through it.\n\n1. In Jarvis tap the three dots, then AI key, then Free key setup. (Or say \"set up smart mode\".)\n\n2. Pick Groq, Gemini or OpenRouter. Each tab has 3 steps and a button to the key page.\n\n3. Paste the key and tap Test. It saves only after Jarvis got an answer.\n\nThe key stays on your phone and is only sent to the company you chose. Never paste it in chat.\n\nOpen it when: you want questions answered that your numbers cannot answer.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the Test result.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Where does your smart mode key live?\n**A:** Only on your phone\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis - talking.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis - talking\n\n> Jarvis speaks every answer out loud by default, and you can talk to it when your phone allows the mic. There is a speaker toggle to turn the voice off.\n\nOn iPhone the first tap unlocks the sound. After that it can speak.\n\nIf the mic is not allowed (common in a Home Screen app) there is a type-to-talk field instead.\n\nThe Voice sheet lists the voices, best first. See [[Phone setup|phone setup]] for the best voice.\n\nOpen it when: your hands are busy.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the speaker toggle, if you want it quiet.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis - the home app.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\", \"tools/web_public/jarvis/\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis - the home app\n\n> Jarvis is your home screen app. You see a greeting, the NQ market line and a card for each of your apps. You can type to it or talk to it.\n\nThe round orb in the corner jumps you to any other OLYMPUS app.\n\nWithout any key it answers from your own numbers: status, room left to the floor, today's P&L, \"open Zenith\".\n\nIt never places a trade. It reads and it opens things.\n\nOpen it when: you start the day, or want any answer fast.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the short list of what needs you first. Urgent things come first.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis and Obsidian notes.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/JARVIS_OBSIDIAN.md\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis and Obsidian notes\n\n> Jarvis reads your Obsidian notes, answers from them and adds to them, but only after you say yes. It is free.\n\nSet up once (about 5 minutes): install the Git plugin in Obsidian, make a private GitHub repo named obsidian-vault, make a token with Contents read and write for that repo.\n\nThen in Jarvis: three dots, Obsidian, paste the repo and the token, Connect.\n\nSay \"what did I write about drawdown\", \"open tasks\" or \"today's daily note\". Say \"note: ...\" to save one. It asks first.\n\nOpen it when: you want to write something down or find something you wrote.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the yes or no question before it saves.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Jarvis modes.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Jarvis modes\n\n> Jarvis has five modes: Auto, Trading, Research, Business and Life. A mode changes the quick questions, which apps show first and how Jarvis thinks.\n\nSwitch with the chips above the quick questions, or say \"switch to research mode\".\n\nAuto (the default) picks the mode from the words of your question.\n\nTrading = the bots, the room left and the plan. Research = Data City, Prometheus, DAPHNE and the Mind. Business = money goal, more accounts, Kalshi, to-dos with deadlines. Life = Planner and reminders.\n\nOpen it when: you switch from trading to studying or planning.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the quick questions change with the mode.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Kalshi - the other bot.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/kalshi/\", \"handoff/ONBOARD.md\"]\n---\n\n# Kalshi - the other bot\n\n> The Kalshi page shows your Kalshi bot's balance, profit, open bets with their edge, the size it uses and how well its odds were right.\n\nThe page only shows numbers the bot's server sends. It is a separate bot with real money, and it lives on its own server.\n\nOpen it when: you want to check the Kalshi bot.\n\nThe one thing to look at: balance and today's profit.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Mind - your notes as a brain.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/mind/\"]\n---\n\n# Mind - your notes as a brain\n\n> Mind shows your Obsidian notes and Jarvis's memory as a living 3D brain.\n\nOpen it when: you want to see what you have been thinking about.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the big clusters: they are the themes of your notes.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/OLYMPUS apps.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [olympus, moc]\n---\n\n# OLYMPUS apps\n\n> How to use each app in one line each. Open the app, look at the one thing the guide names.\n\n- [[Jarvis - the home app]] - Jarvis is your home screen app. You see a greeting, the NQ market line and a card for each of your apps. You can type to it or talk to it.\n- [[Jarvis modes]] - Jarvis has five modes: Auto, Trading, Research, Business and Life. A mode changes the quick questions, which apps show first and how Jarvis thinks.\n- [[Jarvis - Think for me]] - Tap Think for me (or say \"think for me\" or \"what would you do\") and Jarvis gives you 3 ideas of what to do next, most urgent first.\n- [[Jarvis - talking]] - Jarvis speaks every answer out loud by default, and you can talk to it when your phone allows the mic. There is a speaker toggle to turn the voice...\n- [[Jarvis - smart mode (free key)]] - Smart mode makes Jarvis answer anything, not only your numbers. It needs a free key from Groq, Gemini or OpenRouter. The setup screen walks you...\n- [[Jarvis and Obsidian notes]] - Jarvis reads your Obsidian notes, answers from them and adds to them, but only after you say yes. It is free.\n- [[Zenith - the phone dashboard]] - Zenith is the dashboard that runs even when the PC is off. It shows the bots' state, today's result, the equity curve against the drawdown floor and...\n- [[Oracle - the live radar]] - Oracle is a live 3D radar of NQ. It draws the bot's levels, the sweeps, which stage the bot is in and its position.\n- [[Odeon - trade replays]] - Odeon replays the bots' trades as a 3D film, bar by bar, with captions. Each trade is a reel.\n- [[Fates - your odds]] - Fates runs a Monte Carlo (many simulated futures) on the bots' own results and shows how likely an eval is to pass, to fail, and what a month is...\n- [[Data City - tested ideas]] - Data City draws every tested time of day, confluence and market state as a building. It is the machine that tests ideas against the data.\n- [[DAPHNE - the strategy tree]] - DAPHNE searches a tree of rules. Each branch adds one condition to a plain level trade. It cuts branches that cannot win and tests the best path on...\n- [[Prometheus - evolving the bot]] - Prometheus breeds many versions of the live strategy's settings and checks each survivor on data it was not bred on.\n- [[Pantheon - the market mood]] - Pantheon collects free market data (liquidity, rates, volatility, the calendar, the dollar, credit, crypto), one Greek god per source, and ends with...\n- [[Planner - your calendar]] - Planner is your calendar. Jarvis reads it for the day's card and for reminders.\n- [[Mind - your notes as a brain]] - Mind shows your Obsidian notes and Jarvis's memory as a living 3D brain.\n- [[Arena - ranks and labours]] - Arena gives you XP, ranks, 12 Labours, daily quests and streaks. XP comes only from things that can be checked, like the bots running.\n- [[Kalshi - the other bot]] - The Kalshi page shows your Kalshi bot's balance, profit, open bets with their edge, the size it uses and how well its odds were right.\n- [[Tyche - Kalshi as a wheel]] - Tyche draws the Kalshi bot's bets as a wheel of fortune. Open bets orbit as coins. Wins fall into the gold fountain.\n- [[Hermes - your week]] - Hermes (Wrapped) tells the week of the bots as stories: net dollars, dollars a month, the best and worst trade, evals and discipline.\n- [[Bridge - working with ChatGPT]] - Bridge is a page of jobs you can hand to ChatGPT (art, text, bulk work). Claude does the engine, the tests and the publishing.\n- [[Bridge - the ChatGPT sync steps]] - Paste the onboarding text into ChatGPT once. After that ChatGPT and Claude stay in step through one GitHub repo.\n- [[Ares - the war room]] - Ares is the checklist and the risk calculator. You type the entry and the stop and it says GO, WAIT or HALT and how many contracts fit.\n- [[Plutus - the money plan]] - Plutus shows how many prop accounts you could run, what they cost, when payouts land and what a month is worth. Sliders, no promises.\n- [[Athena - this library]] - Athena is this library. Search it, tap a shelf, read the playbooks or take the quiz.\n- [[Phone setup]] - Add each OLYMPUS app to your Home Screen, close and reopen it after an update, and pick the best voice for Jarvis.\n\nJarvis: [[How Jarvis reads this vault]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Odeon - trade replays.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/odeon/\"]\n---\n\n# Odeon - trade replays\n\n> Odeon replays the bots' trades as a 3D film, bar by bar, with captions. Each trade is a reel.\n\nThe card in Jarvis shows the number of reels and the net of all of them.\n\nOpen it when: a trade is done and you want to see how it played out.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the caption on the latest reel: the result in dollars and in [[R]].\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Oracle - the live radar.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/oracle/\"]\n---\n\n# Oracle - the live radar\n\n> Oracle is a live 3D radar of NQ. It draws the bot's levels, the sweeps, which stage the bot is in and its position.\n\nLook at the levels it draws: they are the same levels the strategy marks (see [[Step 1 - mark the levels|step 1 - mark the levels]]).\n\nOpen it when: you want to see what the bot is waiting for right now.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the stage the bot is in, and whether it holds a position.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Pantheon - the market mood.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/pantheon/\", \"docs/PANTHEON.md\"]\n---\n\n# Pantheon - the market mood\n\n> Pantheon collects free market data (liquidity, rates, volatility, the calendar, the dollar, credit, crypto), one Greek god per source, and ends with a Council verdict.\n\nIt is context only. The bot does not use it to trade.\n\nOpen it when: before the session, for the big picture.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the Council verdict, for example Mixed 44 out of 100.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Phone setup.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\", \"docs/JARVIS_APPS.md\"]\n---\n\n# Phone setup\n\n> Add each OLYMPUS app to your Home Screen, close and reopen it after an update, and pick the best voice for Jarvis.\n\n1. Open the page in Safari, tap Share, then Add to Home Screen. Do it for Jarvis, and for any app you use a lot.\n\n2. After an update, fully close OLYMPUS (swipe it away) and reopen it so the new version loads.\n\n3. A white or black screen means the page did not load. Delete the icon and add it again from Safari.\n\n4. Voice tip: Settings, then Accessibility, then Spoken Content, then Voices, then Ava (Premium). Download it and Jarvis sounds better.\n\n5. Each Home Screen app keeps its own storage, so connect the Planner, your key and Obsidian inside Jarvis once.\n\nMain page: https://demetrades2000-coder.github.io/nq-dashboard/ (Jarvis is at /jarvis/).\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Where do you get the best Jarvis voice on iPhone?\n**A:** Settings, Accessibility, Spoken Content, Voices, Ava (Premium)\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Planner - your calendar.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\", \"docs/JARVIS_APPS.md\"]\n---\n\n# Planner - your calendar\n\n> Planner is your calendar. Jarvis reads it for the day's card and for reminders.\n\nOn iPhone each Home Screen app keeps its own storage, so link the Planner's sync once inside Jarvis.\n\nOpen it when: you plan the day or the week.\n\nThe one thing to look at: what is due today.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Plutus - the money plan.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/plutus/\"]\n---\n\n# Plutus - the money plan\n\n> Plutus shows how many prop accounts you could run, what they cost, when payouts land and what a month is worth. Sliders, no promises.\n\n2021 to 2025 is shown as a reference only.\n\nOpen it when: you plan more accounts or wonder about money.\n\nThe one thing to look at: dollars a month on the later half of the data (Jul to Oct).\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Prometheus - evolving the bot.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/prometheus/\"]\n---\n\n# Prometheus - evolving the bot\n\n> Prometheus breeds many versions of the live strategy's settings and checks each survivor on data it was not bred on.\n\nNothing it finds changes the bot by itself.\n\nOpen it when: you wonder if the live settings can be improved.\n\nThe one thing to look at: does the champion still win on the second half (H2). If it drops, the live settings stay.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Tyche - Kalshi as a wheel.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/tyche/\"]\n---\n\n# Tyche - Kalshi as a wheel\n\n> Tyche draws the Kalshi bot's bets as a wheel of fortune. Open bets orbit as coins. Wins fall into the gold fountain.\n\nIt is read-only.\n\nOpen it when: you want a fun look at the Kalshi bot.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the coins that are still open.\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Apps/Zenith - the phone dashboard.md","text":"---\ntype: app-guide\ntags: [olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\", \"docs/ZENITH_COMPARISON.md\"]\n---\n\n# Zenith - the phone dashboard\n\n> Zenith is the dashboard that runs even when the PC is off. It shows the bots' state, today's result, the equity curve against the drawdown floor and the next trading window.\n\nIt is read-only and paper first. It never places or changes a trade.\n\nIt refreshes when the cloud monitor runs, so a number can be a few minutes old.\n\nOpen it when: you want the full picture of the bots, trades and results.\n\nThe one thing to look at: the equity curve and how far it sits above the drawdown floor ([[Drawdown floor|drawdown floor]]).\n\n---\nUp: [[OLYMPUS apps]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Jarvis/How Jarvis reads this vault.md","text":"---\ntype: guide\ntags: [jarvis]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/jarvis/obsidian.js\"]\n---\n\n# How Jarvis reads this vault\n\n> What Jarvis looks at in these notes, so you know where to write things.\n\n- **[[Me]]** (`OLYMPUS/Jarvis/Me.md`): who you are and how to answer. Jarvis reads it to think the way you do.\n- **[[Memory]]** (`OLYMPUS/Jarvis/Memory.md`): lines Jarvis appends when you say \"remember ...\".\n- **Daily notes**: a note named with a date, like `2026-11-04.md`, in the `Daily` folder. Use the [[Daily note]] template.\n- **Tasks**: any line that starts with a checkbox (a dash, a space, an empty box, a space, then the text) is an open task Jarvis can list. Done ones are ticked.\n- **Goals**: any note with *Goals* in its title, tags or a heading. See [[Goals]].\n- **Search**: titles, headings, tags and text. Tags go in the frontmatter `tags: [..]` or as #words.\n- **What Jarvis writes**: only appends, and only into `OLYMPUS/Inbox`, `OLYMPUS/Notes`, `OLYMPUS/Logs`, the two notes above and today's daily note, after you say yes.\n\nThe checklists in this vault are numbered lists on purpose: a list of boxes would show up as open tasks every day.\n\nBack to [[00 Home]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Jarvis/Me.md","text":"---\ntype: me\ntags: [me, jarvis]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/jarvis/obsidian.js\"]\n---\n\n# Me (how Jarvis should think)\n\nJarvis reads this note to prioritize and phrase answers the way I would. Edit it freely; Jarvis only ever appends.\n\n## How I work\n- Money first: say the dollars at risk and the dollars made, then the rest.\n- Results, not talk: short answers, the one action first.\n- Tables when reporting: what was done, the numbers, what is next.\n- I hate repeating manual steps: automate it or fold it into one tap.\n- Quality bar: insane level. If it does not work, say so plainly.\n\n## Trading rules (never break)\n- The stop sits beyond the sweep extreme (the protected high / low), never a fixed distance.\n- Size follows the stop: a wider stop means fewer micros.\n- One clear target, no greed.\n- 2-4 trades a week.\n- Pass evals within 2 weeks; never more than 3.\n- Paper and Sim by default: I arm live myself.\n\n## What I am building: OLYMPUS\n- The NQ trading bots (Zlarior on the MFFU 50K, ATLAS, BLITZ).\n- The Kalshi bot.\n- Ascend (self-improvement).\n- The Planner.\n- Jarvis, the home of it all.\n\n## Where my rules live\n- [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] and [[Risk rules]]\n- [[MFFU rules that matter]] and [[Playbooks]]\n- [[Goals]], [[Daily routine]], [[Templates]]\n- [[00 Home]]\n\n## Preferences Jarvis learned\n"},{"path":"OLYMPUS/Jarvis/Memory.md","text":"---\ntype: memory\ntags: [jarvis, memory]\n---\n\n# Memory\n\nJarvis adds what it learns about you below, one line each. It only ever appends; edit freely.\n\nSee [[Me]] and [[How Jarvis reads this vault]].\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/Daily routine.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, olympus]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\", \"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Daily routine\n\n> A simple day: look before the session, leave the bot alone during it, review after. Three short steps each.\n\nBEFORE the session: 1. Open Jarvis and read the first item on its list. 2. Check the bot is on and its feed is not stale in [[Zenith - the phone dashboard|zenith - the phone dashboard]]. 3. Check days since the last trade (the [[The 7-day rule|the 7-day rule]] rule) and the room left to the [[Drawdown floor|drawdown floor]].\n\nDURING: 1. Let the bot trade. New entries can start from 02:00 to 15:30 New York time. 2. Watch in [[Oracle - the live radar|oracle - the live radar]] if you like, but change nothing. 3. Do not trade by hand without [[Ares - the war room|ares - the war room]] saying GO.\n\nAFTER: 1. Everything is flat by 15:55. Look at the result in [[Zenith - the phone dashboard|zenith - the phone dashboard]]. 2. If a trade closed, watch it in [[Odeon - trade replays|odeon - trade replays]]. 3. Write one line in your notes (say \"note: ...\" to Jarvis) and read tomorrow's plan.\n\nIf something looks wrong, tell the chat. Do not change settings yourself.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/Playbooks.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [playbook, moc]\n---\n\n# Playbooks\n\n> What to do when... Short, numbered, no panic.\n\n- [[When I have a loss]] - A stop-out is a normal cost of the system, not a mistake. Do nothing special: no revenge trade, no bigger size.\n- [[When two losses in a row]] - A short losing run is expected. The system's worst runs in testing were still inside the limits. Keep the size the bot gives you.\n- [[When 7 days with no trade]] - The account can be closed after 7 days with no trade. Watch the calendar in a quiet or holiday week.\n- [[When an eval fails]] - A fail costs the eval fee ($91.80). About 1 eval in 5 fails within 2 weeks by design: the fast size accepts that for speed.\n- [[When an eval passes]] - You hit +$3,000. Stop trading the eval and move the account to funded.\n- [[When I want a payout]] - Check three things before you ask: balance, buffer, best day.\n- [[When I hit the daily loss limit]] - You are paused for the session, not failed. Stop for the day.\n- [[When I want to go live]] - Going live is only ever your decision. The bot is paper only and never arms live trading for you.\n- [[When I want to try a new idea]] - Search the rejected list first. Then test it on both halves with costs and 2 or more trades a week.\n- [[Daily routine]] - A simple day: look before the session, leave the bot alone during it, review after. Three short steps each.\n\nSee also [[Risk rules]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When 7 days with no trade.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# When 7 days with no trade\n\n> The account can be closed after 7 days with no trade. Watch the calendar in a quiet or holiday week.\n\n1. Count days since the last fill. Warn yourself at day 5.\n\n2. Tell the chat at day 5 so it can check for a real setup or a feed problem.\n\n3. Do not force a big trade just to reset the clock. Keep size small and inside the rules.\n\n4. A \"feed stale\" message means the bot will not enter. Fix the feed rather than guessing.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When I have a loss.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# When I have a loss\n\n> A stop-out is a normal cost of the system, not a mistake. Do nothing special: no revenge trade, no bigger size.\n\n1. Check the stop was hit where the rules say (1 tick beyond the [[Sweep|sweep]] extreme). If yes, it worked as designed.\n\n2. Do not change any rule because of one loss or one bad week. Rules change only with two-halves proof.\n\n3. After 2 stop-outs in a week the size halves by itself (outside the eval).\n\n4. If the account is near the [[Drawdown floor|drawdown floor]], look at the room left before the next trade; the bot shrinks size by itself.\n\n5. Tell the chat if something looks off. It checks the trade against the bot's twin.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When I hit the daily loss limit.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# When I hit the daily loss limit\n\n> You are paused for the session, not failed. Stop for the day.\n\n1. Do not try to trade it back.\n\n2. Check that the account is not also close to the floor.\n\n3. Come back next session with the normal size.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When I want a payout.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# When I want a payout\n\n> Check three things before you ask: balance, buffer, best day.\n\n1. Your balance is at least $52,600 for the first payout ($2,100 buffer + $500).\n\n2. Your best day is under 50% of the cycle's profit ([[Consistency rule|consistency rule]]).\n\n3. Ask for between $500 and $2,000. You get 80%.\n\n4. Re-check MFFU's own payout page that day. Rules change.\n\n5. After 5 sim payouts the account moves to live, and you must be present to trade it.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When I want to go live.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, strategy, mffu]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\"]\n---\n\n# When I want to go live\n\n> Going live is only ever your decision. The bot is paper only and never arms live trading for you.\n\n1. MFFU requires you to be present for live trading.\n\n2. Run the Sim test first and read the go-live checklist.\n\n3. Start with the smallest size.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When I want to try a new idea.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# When I want to try a new idea\n\n> Search the rejected list first. Then test it on both halves with costs and 2 or more trades a week.\n\nKeep it only if it is better in 2026 and in 2021 to 2025.\n\nAsk the chat to run it. Do not change live rules by hand.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When an eval fails.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, numbers, mffu]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\", \"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# When an eval fails\n\n> A fail costs the eval fee ($91.80). About 1 eval in 5 fails within 2 weeks by design: the fast size accepts that for speed.\n\n1. Do not panic and do not change the rules. A fail is in the numbers.\n\n2. Buy the next eval (no reset price is published for one-time plans; check the MFFU dashboard).\n\n3. Tell the chat the new start date so the session count starts fresh.\n\n4. Look for a code or fill problem first, but expect it to be normal variance.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When an eval passes.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook, mffu]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# When an eval passes\n\n> You hit +$3,000. Stop trading the eval and move the account to funded.\n\n1. Do not keep trading the eval after the target.\n\n2. Tell the chat so the funded size (60% of the room, at most $1,500) is switched on.\n\n3. Funded rules differ: payouts need a buffer and a best-day check.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Playbooks/When two losses in a row.md","text":"---\ntype: playbook\ntags: [playbook]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# When two losses in a row\n\n> A short losing run is expected. The system's worst runs in testing were still inside the limits. Keep the size the bot gives you.\n\nNever double up to win it back.\n\nNever skip trades by hand because you feel unsure: skipping the one winner is the real cost.\n\n---\nUp: [[Playbooks]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi bot research.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [research, kalshi, moc]\n---\n\n# Kalshi bot research\n\n> Why the Kalshi bot loses money, and the one pocket that might work.\n\n- [[Why the Kalshi bot loses money]] - Kalshi's fee. Before fees the bot is at about +$10 on $21,602 bet (0.05%); the fees are about $73 (0.34% of the money bet).\n- [[Is there a positive pocket on Kalshi]] - Maybe one: entries at 95c and above. 1,075 trades, 98.0% won (97.0% to 98.7%) against 96.9% needed. +$206 on $15,275 (+1.4% per $, range +0.5% to...\n- [[What loses on Kalshi]] - 90-95c entries: -$248 (581 trades, ROI -4.7%, range -7.9% to -1.6%). XRP: -$107 (192 trades, ROI -6.1%).\n- [[Why a 94% win rate still loses]] - A win pays about $0.71, a loss costs about $12.89: one loss erases 18 wins on average (50+ wins at 98c).\n- [[The hidden cause - bet size]] - Losing bets were bigger than winning bets below 97c. At equal size the 90-95c bucket would have lost about $57, it lost $248: $192 came from size.\n- [[Risk of ruin at today's size]] - High at today's size. Late favourite only, 6 contracts (10.5% of bankroll per bet), 90 days: 29% chance to fall under $6 at the observed win rate; 3...\n- [[What to do on Kalshi]] - Keep 15-min favourites off; floor 95c; stop XRP and the alts; shrink to 1-3 contracts (2-5% of bankroll) and size down after losses, not up after...\n- [[How sure is the Kalshi result]] - The 95-99c result has a one-sided p-value of 0.012 before allowing for the fact that I looked at about 14 slices (allowing for that it is roughly...\n\nTemplate: [[Kalshi bet review]]. Long version: [[Kalshi edges - why it loses and what might work]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/How sure is the Kalshi result.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# How sure is the Kalshi result\n\n> The 95-99c result has a one-sided p-value of 0.012 before allowing for the fact that I looked at about 14 slices (allowing for that it is roughly 0.15).\n\nWins are not independent either (coins move together), so the effective sample is smaller than 1,075. Read it as \"plausible, small, worth a forward paper test\", not as \"proven\". The 90-95c loss is stronger evidence (the whole range is below zero) but part of it is bet size, which is a different cause.\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/Is there a positive pocket on Kalshi.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# Is there a positive pocket on Kalshi\n\n> Maybe one: entries at 95c and above. 1,075 trades, 98.0% won (97.0% to 98.7%) against 96.9% needed. +$206 on $15,275 (+1.4% per $, range +0.5% to +2.1%).\n\nThin, and it is the pay for tail risk (see below). Not proven (see \"how sure\").\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Which entry price is the one possibly positive pocket?\n**A:** 95 cents and above\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/Risk of ruin at today's size.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# Risk of ruin at today's size\n\n> High at today's size. Late favourite only, 6 contracts (10.5% of bankroll per bet), 90 days: 29% chance to fall under $6 at the observed win rate; 3 contracts 10%; 1 contract 0.2%.\n\nIf it trades like Sep 23 to Nov 2: 55% to 67% in 90 days.\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/The hidden cause - bet size.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# The hidden cause - bet size\n\n> Losing bets were bigger than winning bets below 97c. At equal size the 90-95c bucket would have lost about $57, it lost $248: $192 came from size.\n\nBets grew with the bankroll ($233 peak on Oct 23) and the worst days came right after (Oct 24: -$102 = -47% in a day).\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/What loses on Kalshi.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# What loses on Kalshi\n\n> 90-95c entries: -$248 (581 trades, ROI -4.7%, range -7.9% to -1.6%). XRP: -$107 (192 trades, ROI -6.1%).\n\n15-min favourites: -$89 (95.0% won, needs 96.6%; already OFF). Under 90c: -$20, unproven.\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/What to do on Kalshi.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# What to do on Kalshi\n\n> Keep 15-min favourites off; floor 95c; stop XRP and the alts; shrink to 1-3 contracts (2-5% of bankroll) and size down after losses, not up after wins.\n\nDetails below.\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/Why a 94% win rate still loses.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# Why a 94% win rate still loses\n\n> A win pays about $0.71, a loss costs about $12.89: one loss erases 18 wins on average (50+ wins at 98c).\n\nBreak-even loss rate is 5.2%, the bot's is 5.5%.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Why does a 94% win rate still lose money on the Kalshi bot?\n**A:** A win pays about $0.71 but a loss costs about $12.89, so one loss erases about 18 wins\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Kalshi/Why the Kalshi bot loses money.md","text":"---\ntype: kalshi\ntags: [kalshi, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# Why the Kalshi bot loses money\n\n> Kalshi's fee. Before fees the bot is at about +$10 on $21,602 bet (0.05%); the fees are about $73 (0.34% of the money bet).\n\nIt buys fairly priced favourites and pays the toll.\n\n---\nUp: [[Kalshi bot research]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [research, moc]\n---\n\n# Rejected ideas\n\n> The graveyard. Search here before you suggest a new strategy idea; do not redo one without a genuinely new idea.\n\n- [[The graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends]] - Many ideas were tested properly and dropped. Before you suggest a new strategy idea, search here first.\n- [[Famous models as taught]] - ICT 2022, Silver Bullet, Mech/IFVG and similar models, traded exactly as taught, made 0.2 to 1.5 trades a week and a PF (profit factor) near 1...\n- [[More confluences (92 tried)]] - 92 extra confirmations, 6 ways to guess the daily bias, and sizing by trade \"grade\" were tested. None held up.\n- [[Fixed small stops]] - Stops of a fixed 10 to 15 points got wicked out and lost money. Real structure stops are rarely that small.\n- [[Other targets]] - No cap, \"draw on liquidity\" and \"last real pool\" targets all did worse (profit factor 1.6 to 2.2 against 3.48).\n- [[Skip after a cold streak]] - A filter that stops trading after losers had the best profit factor, but paused trading for 14 weeks. You rejected it.\n- [[The LIT extras (none adopted)]] - Order blocks, higher-timeframe confluences, other hours, extra continuation trades, looser filters and other markets were tested in hundreds of...\n- [[Fancier exits]] - Exit on a sharp reversal, trailing behind swings, and breakeven at other points: 103 variants, none better in both halves.\n- [[A second strategy alone]] - Breakout and intraday-momentum ideas were real but too weak to pass a prop eval alone. They only work as a small add-on.\n- [[Structure-based management]] - Targets at the next pool, half off early, stops to protected swings, trend filters: 101 variants. None beat the live rules in both halves.\n- [[Skipping LIT to protect a second account]] - Letting the second account (ATLAS) block LIT's opposite trade made the 2021 to 2025 result worse. LIT keeps priority.\n- [[A scalper like a person]] - A fast scalping strategy on its own 25K account, with many exit and filter ideas (about 2,000 variants), did not beat today's rule.\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/A scalper like a person.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# A scalper like a person\n\n> A fast scalping strategy on its own 25K account, with many exit and filter ideas (about 2,000 variants), did not beat today's rule.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/A second strategy alone.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# A second strategy alone\n\n> Breakout and intraday-momentum ideas were real but too weak to pass a prop eval alone. They only work as a small add-on.\n\nProp rules (floor, 50% best day) punish strategies with long losing runs.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Famous models as taught.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Famous models as taught\n\n> ICT 2022, Silver Bullet, Mech/IFVG and similar models, traded exactly as taught, made 0.2 to 1.5 trades a week and a [[PF (profit factor)]] near 1 (break-even).\n\nThat is why the bot has its own rules, written down and tested with costs.\n\nIFVG is explained here: [[IFVG]]. The bot uses a simpler signal.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Fancier exits.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Fancier exits\n\n> Exit on a sharp reversal, trailing behind swings, and [[Breakeven|breakeven]] at other points: 103 variants, none better in both halves.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Fixed small stops.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Fixed small stops\n\n> Stops of a fixed 10 to 15 points got wicked out and lost money. Real structure stops are rarely that small.\n\nYour own principle: the stop sits at the protected low or high, never a random distance.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/More confluences (92 tried).md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# More confluences (92 tried)\n\n> 92 extra confirmations, 6 ways to guess the daily bias, and sizing by trade \"grade\" were tested. None held up.\n\nAdding conditions made trades rarer without making them better. Partials and trailing stops also failed.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Other targets.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Other targets\n\n> No cap, \"draw on liquidity\" and \"last real pool\" targets all did worse (profit factor 1.6 to 2.2 against 3.48).\n\nThe single VIX-band target banked at 8R stayed.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Skip after a cold streak.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Skip after a cold streak\n\n> A filter that stops trading after losers had the best profit factor, but paused trading for 14 weeks. You rejected it.\n\nRule: prefer a size change over skipping. Never pause for weeks.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Skipping LIT to protect a second account.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Skipping LIT to protect a second account\n\n> Letting the second account (ATLAS) block LIT's opposite trade made the 2021 to 2025 result worse. LIT keeps priority.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/Structure-based management.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Structure-based management\n\n> Targets at the next pool, half off early, stops to protected swings, trend filters: 101 variants. None beat the live rules in both halves.\n\nOne lead is being watched (a 15-minute structure trail after +1R) but it is not switched on.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/The LIT extras (none adopted).md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected, strategy]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# The LIT extras (none adopted)\n\n> Order blocks, higher-timeframe confluences, other hours, extra continuation trades, looser filters and other markets were tested in hundreds of variants. None held in both halves.\n\nTwo small upgrades did pass and are ON: the 15-minute check and [[Breakeven|breakeven]] with costs.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Rejected ideas/The graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends.md","text":"---\ntype: rejected\ntags: [research, rejected]\nsource: [\"docs/CLAUDE_REFERENCE.md\"]\n---\n\n# The graveyard - 19 lists of dead ends\n\n> Many ideas were tested properly and dropped. Before you suggest a new strategy idea, search here first.\n\nDo not redo it unless you have a genuinely new idea.\n\nThe rule for keeping an idea: better in 2026 and better in 2021 to 2025, and still 2 or more trades a week.\n\n---\nUp: [[Rejected ideas]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Research hub.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [research, moc]\n---\n\n# Research hub\n\n> What the research nights found, in short notes. The long versions are in `research/writeups/` in the repo.\n\n- [[What we learned]] - one note per research night\n- [[Rejected ideas]] - tested, did not work, and why\n- [[Kalshi bot research]] - why the Kalshi bot loses, the one pocket that might work\n\n## Latest writeups\n- [[Kalshi edges - why it loses and what might work]]\n- [[LIT ideas night - 16 new ideas tested]]\n- [[LIT ideas round 2 - scale-in and six more ideas]]\n- [[Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K]]\n- [[ATLAS research - account B on the Builder 25K]]\n- [[BLITZ research - its own Builder 25K]]\n- [[The money plan research]]\n\nThe rule for keeping an idea: better in 2026 and in 2021-25, and still 2 to 4 trades a week.\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [research, moc]\n---\n\n# What we learned\n\n> Every research night in two lines.\n\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]] - In the support/resistance family 201,908 versions were scored (costs $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, structural stops, no look-ahead).\n- [[A strategy uncorrelated with LIT]] - After reading the rejected list (ORBs with R targets, Gao intraday momentum, gaps, EOD, calendar, fades, continuation), two untested families were...\n- [[Account B - the second strategies on their own MFFU account, Builder]] - EMA + MACD + S/R together on their own MFFU account (\"account B\"), LIT alone on its Builder 50K eval, never opposite to LIT.\n- [[ATLAS + BLITZ edge night]] - 20 hypotheses were written down first, then tested with costs in on CME NQ futures (2026 1m pick on H1 / check on H2, 2021-25 5m reference), random...\n- [[ATLAS - account B (EMA + MACD + S-R) tuned as a whole for the Builder]] - ATLAS was scored under the REAL account-B rules, now including the one the research lacked (no ATLAS entry while LIT has an ARMED setup the other...\n- [[ATLAS edge, day session (b)]] - Two directions (the coordinator moved windows and entry execution to another agent at 13:00) - (1) make last night's paper lead (the stop hunts x0.5...\n- [[ATLAS v2 - making ATLAS (account B, Builder 25K) hold up outside 2026]] - 62 variants (60 pre-declared, 2 added and counted) were scored under the real account-B rules (need size, never opposite LIT incl.\n- [[ATLAS's funded account]] - Once ATLAS's MFFU Builder 25K eval ($43) passes, the bot can now keep trading ATLAS on the new funded account under the funded...\n- [[Bias, filters and timing]] - Two simple biases separate trades in both periods: fading yesterday's candle and trading on the side of last week's midpoint.\n- [[BLITZ (the noise-area scalp) on MFFU]] - Picked on 2026 H1, checked on H2, 2021-25 on 5m bars as the reference; costs as before ($1.90 a micro, 1 tick on every market / stop fill, same-bar...\n- [[Calendar and seasonality]] - No. Across 36 calendar factors (weekday, month, month turn, holidays, OPEX, FOMC/CPI/NFP days, DST and roll weeks, first trade of the week) nothing...\n- [[Cheaper evals - which eval sizing buys a funded account cheapest]] - 13 LIT eval sizings, each eval bought again at once after a fail (MC 1,000 paths, 2026 H1 / H2 and 2021-25).\n- [[Cross-market and regime context]] - Mostly no. VIX level and change, the VXN/VIX ratio, dealer gamma (GEX), NQ-vs-ES relative strength, SMT divergence and the shape of the entry day do...\n- [[Cutting losses to pass more MFFU evals]] - No loss-cut rule makes a big, proven difference.\n- [[Funded-phase money from the payout rules]] - Played on the owner's copied stack (MFFU Builder 50K + 2 x 25K + 4 x 50K-type accounts, all LIT, live eval + funded sizing, a week apart, 12 months...\n- [[HQ LIT - can LIT win more often without losing its payoff Exits, A+]] - The owner asked for 2+ trades a week, high quality, high probability, high profit.\n- [[Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose]] - The clock does not decide the trade (every hour and weekday flips between 2026 and 2021-25); what the trade is made of does.\n- [[Leakage check - does the backtest peek at the future]] - No leak. Switching off the trade memory (memoryseed) and the exit learner (exitlearn) gives exactly the same 768 trades as the live strategy, so...\n- [[LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head]] - Every LIT component the owner listed was built from CME NQ1! 5m data and backtested as a pure LIT strategy (none of the engine's rules).\n- [[LIT + the second strategies]] - 375 combinations were scored on the MFFU Builder 50K (fresh eval every session, costs in), picked on 2026 H1 and checked on H2.\n- [[LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit]] - Every rule of the live LIT was taken away alone (17 components, 46 neighbours / alternatives, 5 eval-cap settings), a greedy \"minimal LIT\" dropped...\n- [[LIT experiments - sizing like a prop trader, a New York session, other]] - About 150 variants of LIT final were tested on 2026 CME NQ1! futures (5m + 1m bars).\n- [[LIT final - one LIT strategy built from the four 2026 studies]] - The four LIT studies (entry, sessions, risk, filters) were merged into one strategy and judged on 2026 only, picked on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct.\n- [[LIT funded accounts on the new live LIT (tcheck + scale-in + BE+1.25)]] - The funded-account studies (lit-funded-firms, lit-mffu-rapid-stack, lit-builder-25k, lit-funded-sizing, the payout habit of lit-money-batch2) were...\n- [[LIT funded sizing - how big to trade once the MFFU eval is passed]] - After the MFFU Builder 50K eval passes, the goal changes from speed to payouts without losing the account.\n- [[LIT higher-time-frame confluences]] - The owner asked for a deep dive into higher-time-frame (HTF) context. Every 2026 LIT setup (330 setups, 176 that would fill alone, 120 taken) got 42...\n- [[LIT loss learning - the 15-minute check, and what the losses can't tell]] - Every 2026 LIT trade was re-played on its true 1-minute path and scored on the number that matters most, the MFFU 50K 2-week pass rate under the live...\n- [[LIT losses - where and why LIT loses in 2026, and the rules worth adding]] - Every 2026 trade of LIT final was taken apart (time, level, sweep, inducement, stop, MFE, news, VIX, ES, opens, 1H/4H).\n- [[LIT on 3 funded accounts]] - Rules were re-checked on 2026-10-19 for the firms whose rules let this bot run (bots allowed, NinjaTrader available, the same bot allowed at other...\n- [[LIT on the 1-minute chart]] - The owner asked for 1-minute entries and loss management that thinks like a person.\n- [[LIT tight stops and bigger size]] - On the exact live LIT signals (120 trades in 2026), 48 stop and entry variants were tested, each at 12 sizes, with the MFFU 50K eval simulated.\n- [[LIT trade selection]] - Every LIT setup of 2026 was collected (330 setups on 167 days, 176 that would have filled if taken alone, against the 120 the bot takes) with 50...\n- [[LIT two-week eval - how to pass the MFFU 50K in 10 sessions or less]] - The owner's order is that an MFFU eval must pass within 2 weeks (10 sessions), so the eval mode was rebuilt for that one number, using the exact live...\n- [[LIT untested entry ideas]] - Four entry ideas nobody had tested on LIT were run on the exact live LIT signals with the live eval sizing, 33 variants, picked on Jan-Jun 2026 and...\n- [[Loss anatomy - where the money leaks]] - In 2021-25 the losses gave back 92% of what the winners made (285R won, 287R lost).\n- [[Managing the trade]] - Today's management is at its sweet spot on all-days rules, with one exception: on trending days (10-day efficiency above 0.45) waiting until +3R to...\n- [[MFFU Builder 25K next to the 50K]] - The owner is buying an MFFU Builder 25K ($63) to trade with the same LIT signals as the Builder 50K eval, with each account sized on its own room.\n- [[Mind - a thinking layer for Zlarior, ATLAS and BLITZ (similar-setup]] - Can think for itself.\" engine/mind.py remembers every historical setup of each bot (631 Zlarior, 1,129 ATLAS, 330 BLITZ) with the features the live...\n- [[MNQ feed vs NQ backtest]] - LIT is backtested on NQ1!, the PC decides on NinjaTrader MNQ. The PC's MNQ bars (27 heartbeat commits) equal TradingView MNQ1! on 95% of bars but are...\n- [[Momentum scalps for a third account]] - 10 momentum / breakout / continuation families (about 10,000 settings, each run on 1m and 5m bars) were tested on CME NQ1!\n- [[Monte Carlo of the MFFU 50K eval]] - One 50K eval on the locked strategy has about a 35% chance of passing within 60 sessions, 30% of failing and 35% of still running (the real history...\n- [[Moving averages as confluence]] - The textbook \"trade with the EMA trend\" filters all cost money (2026 net falls by $5-8k).\n- [[Night R&D (money + eval agent)]] - Three studies, each on the owner's metrics (2026 halves and 2021-25, $ a month net of every eval, MFFU 2-week pass / fail).\n- [[Parameter robustness]] - Mostly a plateau, with one ridge. Cold size, late size, the trending-day breakeven (anything from +3R to never gives the same money), the $ risk and...\n- [[Retail strategies on their own (EMA, MACD, support-resistance incl. ORB]] - The owner asked whether EMA, MACD, support/resistance and supply/demand strategies make money on their own, separate from LIT.\n- [[Risk and sizing - every sizing idea tested on both periods]] - Changing how much the bot risks per trade moves money and drawdown together and does not fix 2021-25.\n- [[Risk summary - size down the two kinds of trades that lose]] - Recommendation: quarter size (not half) in cold streaks and half size on late entries.\n- [[Second strategies on LIT's account]] - The PC bot can now trade the second strategies (EMA London pullback, MACD sweep, S/R NY reclaim) on LIT's own NinjaTrader account next to LIT, one...\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT]] - 192,776 versions of an EMA trend pullback with LIT's tools (sweep, 5m CISD, VIX-band target, BE + costs, 15-minute check) were tested on CME NQ1!\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT (learned)]] - 221,184 versions of two families were tested on CME NQ1! (5m, 1m trade path in 2026), costs $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, structural stops, picked on 2026...\n- [[Sizing cap invariant]] - Pre-launch audit.\n- [[STOP-HUNT audit - trying to break the Asia resweep hunt and the trend]] - An adversarial check of the two stop-hunt leads (stophunt.md) before anyone trusts them, 7 checks.\n- [[STOP-HUNT family - retail stop pools, the hunt, a resweep or inducement]] - Built as its own strategy, any hour 24/5 - 16 retail pool types (1m/5m/15m/1h swings, equal highs/lows, range edges, 3-touch trendlines...\n- [[The eval game - which plan, when to start, two accounts, after a fail]] - The MFFU Builder 50K is the right eval for this strategy (1.24 evals per pass in 2026, 2.52 in 2021-25, about $313 / $1,006 of fees per funded...\n- [[The funded path - from a passed eval to money in the bank]] - Once an MFFU Builder eval passes, the funded account is where the money is.\n- [[The money plan on today's settings (night)]] - MFFU lets one person hold 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25Ks.\n- [[The scaling plan - from 2 accounts (LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K) to 10, when]] - And keep scaling.\" A ladder of five steps (2 / 4 / 6 / 8 / 10 accounts, two accounts a week apart per step) was modelled with the stored studies' own...\n- [[Trade construction]] - One change is worth making, together with the sizing change: on trending days move the stop to breakeven at +3R instead of +1R.\n- [[Uprisk - where the funded money is capped, and what really pays more]] - On the 2026 LIT trades a funded MFFU Builder 50K already makes about $1,950 a month of trading profit, but only about $1,050 a month comes out...\n- [[VWAP and volume profile as confluence]] - None of 24 VWAP and volume-profile ideas made the live strategy better in both 2026 and 2021-25 when used to size trades up or down (every trade...\n- [[What to expect - streaks, drawdowns, flat months and the odds inside]] - The locked strategy wins 1 trade in 4-5 and loses or scratches the rest, so long runs without a winner are normal: 33 trades in a row without a win...\n- [[Win rate and more trades]] - More trades from new windows or second trades lose money in 2021-25 every time (London, Asia, pre-open, lunch, late PM, 2 a day), even at a quarter...\n- [[Zlarior eval size worked out state by state (dynamic programming,]] - The live eval size is one rule for every eval state (micros so one winner covers what is left to +$3,000, capped at 80% of the room).\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/A second strategy next to LIT.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-sr.md\"]\n---\n\n# A second strategy next to LIT\n\n> In the support/resistance family 201,908 versions were scored (costs $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, structural stops, no look-ahead).\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-22.\n\nOne survives - the NY sweep-and-reclaim of the day's levels (prior-day, overnight, Asia, London, prior-week, opening range; 09:30-12:00; inducement first; reclaim close with a 0.3 ATR body; stop beyond the sweep extreme; 2R; LIT's 15-minute check).\n\nAt $500 risk a trade - 2026 H1 +$12.2k (PF 3.72), H2 (untouched) +$2.9k (PF 1.89), 2025 +$5.0k (PF 1.26), 2021-24 +$4.8k (PF 1.06); about 1.1-1.6 trades a week; daily correlation with LIT -0.05 in 2026.\n\nOn one account with LIT (LIT priority, max 2 a day) 2026 goes from +$43.4k to +$58.0k with a smaller max DD (-$1,767 vs -$2,196). The edge outside 2026 is thin and leans on a few winners; the break-and-retest family did not survive.\n\nAlso found - the earlier retail study's S/R level targets used the overnight high/low before 09:30 (look-ahead); fixed here.\n\nWords used here: [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[ATR]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/A strategy uncorrelated with LIT.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/diversifier-search.md\"]\n---\n\n# A strategy uncorrelated with LIT\n\n> After reading the rejected list (ORBs with R targets, Gao intraday momentum, gaps, EOD, calendar, fades, continuation), two untested families were built on CME NQ1!\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-26.\n\n5m - (1) the noise-area intraday momentum of Zarattini, Aziz & Barbon 2024 (trade a half-hour close outside the day's time-of-day noise band from the open, stop at the band / VWAP, flat 15:55) and (2) a VXN-normalised initial-balance breakout held for the day.\n\nThe IB family is dead (0 of 24 settings positive in all four spans).\n\nThe noise-area family is real - the paper's own rule, untouched, makes money in every span (H1 +$3.5k, H2 +$14.8k, 2025 +$2.7k, 2021-24 +$68.5k at $500 a trade) but trades 5.5 a week; the joint pick (H1 + 2021-24, band 1.25 sigma) makes H1 +$9.1k (PF 1.28), H2 +$16.1k (PF 2.01), 2025 +$12.1k (PF 1.21), 2021-24...\n\nBut it is a trend strategy - 26-30% winners, 18-23R drawdowns, the 5 best trades carry each 2026 half - and the MFFU rules choke it - alone it passes 0-22% of evals in 2 weeks (LIT 51-57%) and a funded account pays it $13-117 a month (the 50% best-day rule and the $2,000 floor).\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/ATLAS + BLITZ edge night.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\ndate: 2026-10-30\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas-blitz-edge-2026-10-30.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS + BLITZ edge night\n\n> 20 hypotheses were written down first, then tested with costs in on CME NQ futures (2026 1m pick on H1 / check on H2, 2021-25 5m reference), random controls, neighbours and per-year checks.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-30.\n\nBLITZ - one sizing change holds - on the 25K eval, the day's FIRST band break on a side trades half size and a LATER same-side break 1.75 x today's size (never more than the room). Same trades.\n\n2-week pass / fail 47.9 / 23.5 (today 36.1 / 37.8), 36.2 / 21.7 (36.2 / 34.8), 24.7 / 48.4 (20.4 / 50.2) in H1 / H2 / 2021-25; whole 25K slot $286 / $52 / -$39 a month (today $270 / $37 / -$40); 54 of 54 room-capped neighbours do the same; shuffled sizes reach it in 0-7.5% of draws; random directions with the same...\n\nWeak spots - H2 decides fewer evals in 2 weeks (58% vs 71%: fewer fail), a later break can risk up to $1,050, and the 25K slot still loses ~$40 a month in 2021-25.\n\nRejected - trend-day size from the range so far / first hour / overnight range / gap / VIX change / 2-sigma excursion / VWAP (430 tilts, survivors at chance), the 10:30 check (H1 slot $270 -> $54). BLITZ's 2021-25 edge is gone at +1 tick a side.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/ATLAS - account B (EMA + MACD + S-R) tuned as a whole for the Builder.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS - account B (EMA + MACD + S-R) tuned as a whole for the Builder\n\n> ATLAS was scored under the REAL account-B rules, now including the one the research lacked (no ATLAS entry while LIT has an ARMED setup the other way).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-22.\n\nWith it, today's ATLAS on a Builder 25K passes 72.3% of evals within 2 weeks in 2026 H1 (0.0% fail, 5.0% stuck) and 43.5% in H2 (0.0 / 0.0), eval-path +$19,737 ($3,238/mo) and +$5,448 ($1,467/mo), about $1,840 paid out in the first 3 funded months (H1 starts), +$1,549 per $63 eval bought.\n\n358 tweaks were tested (members, Asia slot, per-member size weights down AND up, priority, 25K sizing incl. room 40-60% / safe / hybrid / daily cap, entry windows, weekdays, VIX filters and VIX size, per-member exits, a latency-proof limit entry) and picked on H1, checked on H2.\n\nOnly 5 different ones beat today in both halves, about what chance gives (3.1), and none is a plateau - the best, S/R entries only until 11:00, wins H1 only by skipping three 11:00 stop-outs (11:05 gives today's H1 exactly). The limit entry does not fix the late-entry weakness and costs about a third of the money.\n\nThe confluence job's 2 hand-off candidates (EMA big-range-day skip, EMA 15-minute check) fail this rule too. Fixed on the way - account B's size now never lets one stop-out exceed the rest of the soft daily loss (no change on the 25K).\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/ATLAS edge, day session (b).md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\ndate: 2026-10-30\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas-edge-2026-10-30b.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS edge, day session (b)\n\n> Two directions (the coordinator moved windows and entry execution to another agent at 13:00) - (1) make last night's paper lead (the stop hunts x0.5 + the NA day-hold member x0.5, run abatlasnahuntslead) pass every gate, (2) funded-stage money.\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-30.\n\nNew tool - a whole-account SLOT simulator (eval -> funded -> MFFU payouts -> a new $63 eval; 300 block-bootstrap paths x 6 months per span) that measures $ to the owner a month net of every eval bought, next to the eval-path $ and the 25K 2-week pass / dead.\n\nMain finding - the lead's extra money was profit INSIDE evals, which is never paid; on the funded account the NA member gets 28-32% of accounts stuck in 6 months (hunts alone 3-9%), so the lead pays the owner LESS than today in H1 ($532 vs $568 a month).\n\nIt can be made to pass every gate (NA only from eval session 11 / only while the room is >= $1,500, never once funded - 20 of 33 neighbours pass), but then it pays the same as the hunts alone (0 of 33 beat them).\n\nThe money is in the hunts, and most of it on the funded account - eval = today's ATLAS exactly, once funded + shasia + shtrend at x0.5 - slot $661 / $510 / $23 a month (H1 / H2 / 2021-25) vs today $568 / $382 / -$2; every funded weight 0.4-1.0 passes; 12 random-time twins of the hunts pass 0 times (their H2 slot...\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/ATLAS v2 - making ATLAS (account B, Builder 25K) hold up outside 2026.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas-v2.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS v2 - making ATLAS (account B, Builder 25K) hold up outside 2026\n\n> 62 variants (60 pre-declared, 2 added and counted) were scored under the real account-B rules (need size, never opposite LIT incl.\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-25.\n\nLIT's armed setups, $600 soft daily loss, stuck under $100), picked on 2026 H1, checked on H2, and the 5-year reference had to not lose. Money = the eval path less the $63 fees, averaged over paths started every 5th session (the single published path hangs on one start).\n\nToday - H1 2-week pass 65.5% / 0 fails, +$8,500 ($1,417/mo); H2 43.5% / 0, +$2,029 ($580/mo); 2025 -$1,935; 2021-24 -$1,429.\n\nBest lead - add the stop-hunt candidates shasia + shtrend as members at half size - H1 67.2% / 0, +$10,090 ($1,682/mo); H2 55.1% / 0, +$3,260 ($931/mo); 2025 -$854 (still negative, but half the loss); 2021-24 +$2,542.\n\nIt holds as a plateau (sizes 0.4-0.75 all better in both 2026 halves and in 2021-24), it beats 20 random-time placebo members in H2 and 2021-24 (p 0.0-0.1), its flipped twin is worse everywhere, and without each member's 3 best trades it keeps H2 at 24.6% / +$819 where today falls to 11.6% / -$326.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/ATLAS's funded account.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas-funded.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS's funded account\n\n> Once ATLAS's MFFU Builder 25K eval ($43) passes, the bot can now keep trading ATLAS on the new funded account under the funded rules (one owner-run switch, never automatic).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-26.\n\nReplayed on ATLAS's real trades under the account-B rules (no hedging against LIT, LIT's armed setups block it), with the live funded sizing (60% of the room, at most $750 a trade, a winner at most $1,000) and MFFU's payout rules, a funded ATLAS 25K pays you about $517 a month on the Jul-Oct check half ($582...\n\nIn a 2025 or 2021-24 market it pays only $64 / $39 a month and 89-96% of funded accounts get stuck within 6 months, because ATLAS has almost no edge there. Paper only; nothing is armed.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Account B - the second strategies on their own MFFU account, Builder.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-account-b.md\"]\n---\n\n# Account B - the second strategies on their own MFFU account, Builder\n\n> EMA + MACD + S/R together on their own MFFU account (\"account B\"), LIT alone on its Builder 50K eval, never opposite to LIT.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-22.\n\nScored on both plan sizes with the plan's own rules and the size the bot routes (LIT's twoweek \"one winner passes\" on that plan), picked on 2026 H1, checked on H2. Builder 50K - 2-week pass/fail 72.3/2.5 (H1) and 53.6/0.0 (H2), eval path +$44.4k / +$7.6k, funded about $3,760 in 3 months, +$3,347 per eval bought.\n\nBuilder 25K - 68.9/0.0 and 43.5/0.0, path +$19.7k / +$5.4k, funded about $1,840, +$1,549 per eval, never a hard fail in 2026, at most $477 at risk a trade (50K $940).\n\nThe 50K makes about twice the money, but MFFU allows only ONE sim-funded Builder 50K per person (LIT's eval takes it once funded) and up to TWO sim-funded Builder 25Ks; no evaluation limit was found and nothing found forbids a 25K next to a 50K.\n\nVerdict - buy a Builder 25K ($63) for account B; switch to a 50K only if MFFU confirms in writing that two funded 50Ks are fine. Honest catch - a 2026 bet; in 2025 and 2021-24 a third of account B evals die within 2 weeks (mostly stuck under $100 of room) and $ per eval bought is about $0-60.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/BLITZ (the noise-area scalp) on MFFU.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/scalp-na-mffu.md\"]\n---\n\n# BLITZ (the noise-area scalp) on MFFU\n\n> Picked on 2026 H1, checked on H2, 2021-25 on 5m bars as the reference; costs as before ($1.90 a micro, 1 tick on every market / stop fill, same-bar tie = loss).\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-28.\n\n93 filters + 90 filter pairs, 4,800 exits, 896 eval / funded sizes, 1,536 whole-account slots, 22 ATLAS and 121 LIT combinations.\n\nOne filter holds - trade only when yesterday's 09:30-16:00 range was at least 0.8 x its 14-day average - avg R a trade +0.098 -> +0.150 (H1), +0.066 -> +0.171 (H2), +0.007 -> +0.016 (2021-25); at $150 a trade PF 1.63 / 1.49 / 1.06, +$912 / +$577 / +$89 a month, 7-9 trades a week; random directions lose (p 0.025 /...\n\nNo exit change, daily profit stop or \"need\" pass-maximizer holds. Alone on an MFFU 25K the eval passes 31-35% in 2 weeks (2021-25 17%) but the whole account slot pays $224 / $3 / -$40 a month (H1 / H2 / 2021-25).\n\nInside ATLAS's 25K it lifts H2 (2-week pass 43.5 -> 55.1%) but adds dead evals (H1 0.8 -> 3.4%, 2021-24 27 -> 36%) and only ~$25-35 a month funded.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Bias, filters and timing.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/bias-filters.md\"]\n---\n\n# Bias, filters and timing\n\n> Two simple biases separate trades in both periods: fading yesterday's candle and trading on the side of last week's midpoint.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nAs filters or score points they cut trades below 2 a week and the replacement trades lose; as a size-down (70% size when both disagree) they raise PF in both periods (2026 3.48 to 3.62, 2021-25 1.09 to 1.12) and cut 2021-25 drawdown, for −$0.75k in 2026. No filter or timing change beats today's rules.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Calendar and seasonality.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/calendar-effects.md\"]\n---\n\n# Calendar and seasonality\n\n> No. Across 36 calendar factors (weekday, month, month turn, holidays, OPEX, FOMC/CPI/NFP days, DST and roll weeks, first trade of the week) nothing holds in both 2026 and 2021-25 beyond what chance produces.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe only rule that is better in both periods, half size the day after NFP, gains $748 over 5 years and $256 in 2026 on 3 trades; a random group of the same size does as well 15% of the time. Keep trading every day at the normal size.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Cheaper evals - which eval sizing buys a funded account cheapest.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/eval-cost.md\"]\n---\n\n# Cheaper evals - which eval sizing buys a funded account cheapest\n\n> 13 LIT eval sizings, each eval bought again at once after a fail (MC 1,000 paths, 2026 H1 / H2 and 2021-25).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-26.\n\nThe fee is small next to time - a funded 50K is worth about $40 a session - so the cheapest sizing per funded account (room 40-55%) is the slowest and pays least.\n\nOne change holds in H1, H2 and 2021-25, on the 50K and the 25K and at every price - keep the twoweek sizing after session 10 instead of dropping to safeeval (twoweekuntil 999, no reset).\n\nExact MFFU slot money, 6 months, net of every eval - 50K $837 / $818 / +$7 a month vs $823 / $798 / -$7 today (H1 / H2 / 2021-25); 25K at $63 $383 / $352 / -$9 vs $375 / $331 / -$15. Funded within 20 sessions H2 85% vs 78%, 2021-25 40% vs 34%; evals per funded 50K in 2021-25 2.77 vs 3.25 ($254 vs $298).\n\nThe 2-week pass/fail is unchanged (52.9 / 17.8, 58.6 / 20.9), slow passes fall (H2 13.8% -> 8.8%), fails within 20 sessions rise 3-4 points. Resets (a new eval at session 10 / 15 / 20) are no better and lose in 2021-25 - keep \"no reset\". Paper only; nothing live changed.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Cross-market and regime context.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/cross-market-context.md\"]\n---\n\n# Cross-market and regime context\n\n> Mostly no. VIX level and change, the VXN/VIX ratio, dealer gamma (GEX), NQ-vs-ES relative strength, SMT divergence and the shape of the entry day do not split trades in a way that holds in both 2026 and 2021-25; 25 of 32 size rules were rejected.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-17.\n\nOne small thing does hold: when BOTH the 10-day and the 20-day trend reading say \"trending\" (12 trades in 2026, 75 in 2021-25), the reversal loses money in every period; half size there adds +$378 in 2026 (PF 3.94 to 4.16, drawdown -$2,249 to -$1,871) and +$677 in 2021-25 (PF 1.27 to 1.29).\n\nThe bigger lead is that the 20-day trend test picks the bad days better than the live 10-day test (swap: 2026 +$2.1k, 2021-25 +$5.6k, but more 1-month eval fails); it needs an engine option and a longer live data window before it can be tested exactly.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Cutting losses to pass more MFFU evals.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/loss-cut-eval-pass.md\"]\n---\n\n# Cutting losses to pass more MFFU evals\n\n> No loss-cut rule makes a big, proven difference.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe best new idea, half size for the rest of the eval week after two losing trades in that week, lifts the pass rate of decided evals from 40% to 42% (2021-25) and 53% to 56% (5 years), makes a little more money in both periods (2026 +$289, 2021-25 +$828) and cuts 2021-25 1-month fails from 5.2% to 3.1%.\n\nIts neighbours agree, but the trades it shrinks are not worse than average, so it is a small safety rule, not an edge. The intraday package does not survive the critic: CISD x1.25 lowers the pass rate, and the 30-40 pt and equal-highs slices flip by year or by odd/even month.\n\nThe math: with a 21% win rate, a +$542 average win and a -$114 average loss, 2021-25 passes about 40% of decided evals at today's size. 70-80% is only reachable in a 2026-like market (91% today), or by trading so small that one eval takes 6-12 months.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Funded-phase money from the payout rules.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/payout-policy.md\"]\n---\n\n# Funded-phase money from the payout rules\n\n> Played on the owner's copied stack (MFFU Builder 50K + 2 x 25K + 4 x 50K-type accounts, all LIT, live eval + funded sizing, a week apart, 12 months on the same market path, net of every eval; MC 1,000 paths, 5- and 21-session blocks).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-26.\n\nOne mechanic pays - buy the replacement eval when an account takes its 4th payout instead of after its 5th (MFFU / LucidPro / FundedNext all end an account after 5), so the next funded account is ready when the old one leaves - +$232 a month on the stack in both 2026 halves (5-session blocks; +$188 to +$270 with...\n\nRejected - the ask line ($1,000 or $2,000 above the buffer instead of $1,500: -$36 to +$9 a month), consaware on the live funded sizing (-$566 / -$223 a month H1 / H2, more dry months), and a stack payout reserve (at most 2-4 payouts a month across the accounts so a ready account waits for an empty month - dry...\n\nPaper only; nothing live changed.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/HQ LIT - can LIT win more often without losing its payoff Exits, A+.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/hq-lit.md\"]\n---\n\n# HQ LIT - can LIT win more often without losing its payoff Exits, A+\n\n> The owner asked for 2+ trades a week, high quality, high probability, high profit.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-23.\n\nThree things were tested on today's live LIT (2026 H1 pick, H2 check, 2021-25 reference, $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, eval path = live twoweek sizing). (1) Exits, 93 variants on the engine's own trades, re-walked exit for exit (631/631 equal to the engine). That includes the partial at 1R that could not be measured before.\n\nNo exit raises the win rate without losing money. A 50% partial at 1R costs 37% of H1 and 42% of H2 ($21.4k / $5.4k vs $33.7k / $9.4k) and drops the H2 2-week eval pass from 52% to 15%. Not one trade moves above +0.5R, because the runner then mostly stops at breakeven.\n\nTrailing, earlier or later breakeven, structure breakeven and time exits all lose money or eval odds. LIT's edge is the far VIX-band target, and the live exits are already the best of the 93 in H1.\n\n(2) An A+/B/C grade from facts known at the fill: a pre-registered textbook checklist, a grade fitted on H1, and the single best H1 feature. None beat a random-grade placebo in H2 at 95%. Each one reverses in 2021-25.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/intraday-anatomy.md\"]\n---\n\n# Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose\n\n> The clock does not decide the trade (every hour and weekday flips between 2026 and 2021-25); what the trade is made of does.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nCISD triggers beat IFVG triggers in both periods (2026 PF 5.7 vs 2.4, 2021-25 PF 1.53 vs 1.11), 30-40 pt stops and equal-highs/lows targets lose money in both.\n\nHalving the 30-40 pt stops is the one clean small win (2026 +$413, 2021-25 +$115, max drawdown -$9.3k to -$8.1k, 1-month eval fails 5.2% to 4.0%); adding CISD x1.25 and half size on equal-highs targets makes 2026 +$3.7k and 2021-25 +$1.4k with less drawdown in both, but it is three in-sample slices stacked, so it...\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[IFVG]], [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-strategy.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head\n\n> Every LIT component the owner listed was built from CME NQ1! 5m data and backtested as a pure LIT strategy (none of the engine's rules).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-18.\n\nTaken raw, LIT breaks even (PF 0.97 in 2026, 1.01 in 2021-25). The best rule set (strong displacement + deep sweep + previous day/week/month targets) makes money in both periods (2026 PF 1.26, +$5.3k; 2021-25 PF 1.15, +$31.5k) and holds up in every check, but it is far behind the locked bot in 2026 (+$20.5k, PF 3.94).\n\nIts 21% losing-trade drawdown makes the MFFU 50K eval fail more often than it passes. Verdict: LIT does not beat the current bot. Five of the owner's components carry a small edge in both periods; the rest do not.\n\nWords used here: [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[Displacement|displacement]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT + the second strategies.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-mix.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT + the second strategies\n\n> 375 combinations were scored on the MFFU Builder 50K (fresh eval every session, costs in), picked on 2026 H1 and checked on H2.\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-22.\n\nThe LIT eval path was reproduced exactly first (57.1/5.9 H1, 52.2/0.0 H2).\n\nPutting second strategies on LIT's own eval does not hold up - the best H1 pick (LIT + MACD at LIT's size, 2-week pass 81.5%) falls below LIT alone in H2 (46.4% vs 52.2%), none of the top 20 H1 picks beats LIT in H2, and they block 12-15 LIT trades a half.\n\nOnly LIT + EMA + Asia beats LIT in both halves (61.3/2.5 and 66.7/0.0) but needs Asia entries the PC route blocks and fails 24% of 2025 evals (LIT 1%).\n\nThe better setup is two accounts - LIT alone on its eval, and EMA + MACD + S/R together on a second Builder 50K ($91.80) at LIT's two-week \"need\" size with a no-hedge guard - 2-week pass/fail 72.3/4.2 in H1 and 53.6/0.0 in H2, both accounts together +$76k / +$17k on the eval paths vs +$34k / +$9k.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-ablation.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit\n\n> Every rule of the live LIT was taken away alone (17 components, 46 neighbours / alternatives, 5 eval-cap settings), a greedy \"minimal LIT\" dropped whatever cost nothing in 2026 H1, 22 numeric knobs got a sensitivity map, a sampled Shapley split shows...\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-23.\n\nFour rules carry LIT - the displacement filter (without it H2 is -$1.3k instead of +$9.5k), the 1m CISD entry, the VIX-band target and the inducement - plus the Monday rule and the 15-minute check on the eval path.\n\nTwo rules do nothing on the live eval: the weekly stop (twoweek sizing ignores it) and the $1,000 soft-daily-loss cap (never binds under the 60% room cap). A simpler LIT without the weekly stop, the 1.5R floor, the 15:55 flat and the BE costs holds up as well (H1 +$34.9k, H2 +$9.6k, eval 2-week 57.1/6.7 and 52.2/0.0).\n\nSpiky knobs: displacement 0.8, the 8R bank, inducement distance 1.0, the 02:00 start and the 1m CISD window 20 (one bar from a cliff).\n\nRecommended for approval - (1) cisd1m 20 -> 22 (same H1 to the dollar, H2 +$426, eval unchanged: insurance off a cliff), (2) optional twoweek room cap 60% -> 55% (H1 2-week fails 5.9% -> 2.5%, H2 2-week unchanged, H2 1-month fails 0 -> 1.7%).\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[Displacement|displacement]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT experiments - sizing like a prop trader, a New York session, other.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-experiments.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT experiments - sizing like a prop trader, a New York session, other\n\n> About 150 variants of LIT final were tested on 2026 CME NQ1! futures (5m + 1m bars).\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-18.\n\nEach was picked on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct, with 2021-25 as a reference. None of the strategy changes beat LIT final in both halves. That covers a New York second session, 15 target ideas, 5 stop anchors with 4 buffers, breakeven on structure, partials at liquidity, re-entries, minimum 3R and a two-stage entry.\n\nThe only real gains come from sizing for the MFFU 50K eval. (1) Fast-eval size: $800 risk up to a 40-pt stop instead of 20. It passes about half of all evals within 2 weeks in both halves (H1 52% vs 33%, H2 47% vs 23%) and halves the expected time to a pass, but about 1 eval in 4 fails within 2 weeks.\n\n(2) A max-loss room cap: never risk more than 60% of the distance to the trailing max-loss floor. It cuts 2-week failures from 15% to 4% in H1 and leaves H2 unchanged. Paper only.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT final - one LIT strategy built from the four 2026 studies.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-final.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT final - one LIT strategy built from the four 2026 studies\n\n> The four LIT studies (entry, sessions, risk, filters) were merged into one strategy and judged on 2026 only, picked on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct.\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-18.\n\nThe final, LIT final, combines the entry study's CISD entry with a 1-minute confirmation and the risk study's VIX-band target banked at 8R. In 2026 it makes +$38,873 (PF 3.06, 3.1 trades a week, max DD -$3,097), against +$20,487 for the old bot.\n\nAlmost all of the extra money comes from Jan-Jun, the half the parts were tuned on. In Jul-Oct it makes +$8,212 (PF 2.19), while the old bot made +$15,568 over the same months. It only works on true 1-minute bars, most trades come in the London morning (02:00-08:00 ET), and it has no edge in 2021-25 (PF 1.03).\n\nPaper only. Since 2026-10-18 it is the paper bot's strategy (livemodel.json, engine/litlive.py; the old bot runs on as a shadow ledger).\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT funded accounts on the new live LIT (tcheck + scale-in + BE+1.25).md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-funded-combo.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT funded accounts on the new live LIT (tcheck + scale-in + BE+1.25)\n\n> The funded-account studies (lit-funded-firms, lit-mffu-rapid-stack, lit-builder-25k, lit-funded-sizing, the payout habit of lit-money-batch2) were re-run on the trades of the new live LIT, with the 15-minute check, one scale-in add after breakeven and...\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-20.\n\nEvery account sizes its adds with the live rule, capped by its own max contracts. Where a best-day rule applies, the adds are trimmed so the day's win stays inside the cap.\n\nThe current plan (MFFU Builder 50K + LucidPro 50K + FundedNext Rapid Daily 50K, $377 up front) makes $2,147 a month over 6 months net of every fee on Jul-Oct trades (H2, the number to quote; $2,655 on Jan-Jun), from the study of 2026-11-04 05:27 with the live presets.\n\nMonths 4-6 make $2,792 (Jul-Oct) and $3,292 (Jan-Jun). The 6 months lose money in 5.2% of paths (Jul-Oct). Each account loses its funded account in 6 months 0.9-1.5% of the time. Adding a Builder 25K one week later adds about $290 a month, and a second LucidPro a week later adds about $840.\n\nA Rapid EOD adds about $1,265 a month, but it needs MFFU's written OK, its eval takes about 2 months and the funded news ban costs it a third of the Jul-Oct points.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT funded sizing - how big to trade once the MFFU eval is passed.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-funded-sizing.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT funded sizing - how big to trade once the MFFU eval is passed\n\n> After the MFFU Builder 50K eval passes, the goal changes from speed to payouts without losing the account.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nThe exact live LIT trades of 2026 were replayed on a modelled MFFU funded account (EOD trailing $2,000 locking at +$100, $1,000 soft daily loss, payouts above a $2,100 buffer, $500-$2,000 a request, best day at most 50% of the cycle, 5 sim payouts, 80% split).\n\nKeeping today's normal or eval size on a funded account loses 20-22% (Jan-Jun) / 28-29% (Jul-Oct) of accounts within 6 months.\n\nThe new mode \"funded\" risks 40% of the room to the floor (at most $800, so a fresh account trades the normal LIT size), sizes so a winner pays at most $1,500 (the 50% best-day payout rule), keeps the weekly stop, and skips a trade when even 1 micro could reach the floor.\n\nIt loses 1.0% / 1.5% of accounts in 6 months and pays $4,613 / $4,433 in 6 months ($769 / $739 a month) vs $3,423 / $3,020 for normal size ($1.90 commission). The best-day cap is the lever that matters; fixed $ tiers, fractional Kelly and % of the room all sit on the same plateau once it is on.\n\nWords used here: [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT higher-time-frame confluences.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-htf-confluences.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT higher-time-frame confluences\n\n> The owner asked for a deep dive into higher-time-frame (HTF) context. Every 2026 LIT setup (330 setups, 176 that would fill alone, 120 taken) got 42 causal HTF facts, read as 49 HTF confluences (1H, 4H, daily and weekly candles built from the CME NQ1!\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-18.\n\n5m file; look-ahead test 30 of 30 identical) plus 26 LIT facts. 75 confluences were scored in Jan-Jun (pick) and Jul-Oct (check), with permutation p-values and a multiple-testing correction. Nothing survives the correction, and the number that \"hold in both halves\" (18 of 65) is exactly what chance gives (17.7).\n\nThe owner's main idea, trading WITH the HTF draw, flips between the halves and loses money as a filter, as a London-only filter and as a size rule. 86,000 2- and 3-way combinations were tried: the HTF-only ones picked on Jan-Jun held in Jul-Oct 0 times out of 22. Skipping setups never works (below 2 trades a week).\n\nThree small size tilts help both halves and are adopt-candidates at low confidence. (1) The 4H draw, size-neutral: 1.4x when the nearest unswept 4H swing is on the target side, else 0.8x.\n\nIt is the only rule that raises the 2-week pass in both halves (H1 37.5% -> 42.2%, H2 29.5% -> 34.6%; cost per 2-week pass $245 -> $218 and $311 -> $265), but Jul-Oct 1-month fails go from 0% to 10.3%. (2) The fresh trap times a clear HTF path: H1 2-week pass 52.3%, H2 unchanged at 29.5%, +$10.3k / +$1.5k.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT loss learning - the 15-minute check, and what the losses can't tell.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-loss-learning.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT loss learning - the 15-minute check, and what the losses can't tell\n\n> Every 2026 LIT trade was re-played on its true 1-minute path and scored on the number that matters most, the MFFU 50K 2-week pass rate under the live two-week sizing (config C).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nOne rule holds in both halves. The 15-minute check: 15 minutes after the fill, if the trade has never been +0.5R in profit and the 1m close is more than 0.3R below the entry, get out at the next 1m open.\n\nIt cut 13 trades in 2026, all of them would-be full stop-outs, at about -0.45R instead of -1R, and touched no winner. 2-week pass H1 52.9% -> 57.1% and H2 50.7% -> 52.2%; 2-week fails 8.4% -> 5.9% and 2.9% -> 0%; the Monte Carlo agrees in both halves; eval-path money +$4.4k (H1) and +$5.0k (H2).\n\nIts neighbours (0.25-0.4R, +0.3-0.7R, minute 15-20) agree. Checking earlier (5-12 min) or later (25-30 min), or keeping the check on after minute 15, kills slow winners in Jul-Oct.\n\nEverything else failed: wider stops (stop-outs are not stop hunts), tightening the stop with time, scratching after a small profit, sequence memory (after a loss in the same direction, the same level type or the day before), and a walk-forward context model (no skill out of sample).\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT losses - where and why LIT loses in 2026, and the rules worth adding.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-losses.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT losses - where and why LIT loses in 2026, and the rules worth adding\n\n> Every 2026 trade of LIT final was taken apart (time, level, sweep, inducement, stop, MFE, news, VIX, ES, opens, 1H/4H).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\n59 stop-outs carry 97% of the lost dollars. They are trades that never worked (median best point +0.3R, 45 minutes to the stop), not shake-outs. About 110 rules were tested, each picked on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct, plus timeframes and the masters-study filters.\n\nTwo rules help both halves and the eval: a weekly stop (after 2 stop-outs in a week, half size for the rest of it) and a Monday rule (once Monday has taken Friday's high, only shorts; once it has taken Friday's low, only longs).\n\nTogether (litlosspack): 2026 +$41.7k, PF 3.66, max DD -$2.3k, eval pass rate 95.5% (was 80%), H1 +$2.1k and H2 +$0.75k better than LIT final. Every exit-management idea (scale-outs, trails, stop to the inducement) and every other timeframe lost money in both halves.\n\nWords used here: [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT on 3 funded accounts.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-funded-firms.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT on 3 funded accounts\n\n> Rules were re-checked on 2026-10-19 for the firms whose rules let this bot run (bots allowed, NinjaTrader available, the same bot allowed at other firms).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-19.\n\nEach plan was played end to end on the exact live LIT trades of 2026 with an eval sizing and a funded sizing tuned to that plan's rules on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct. Recommended: keep the MFFU Builder 50K and add a LucidPro 50K and a FundedNext Rapid Daily 50K.\n\nOver 6 months from the purchase, net of every fee, the three pay on average $2,655 a month on Jan-Jun 2026 trades and $2,147 on Jul-Oct (months 4-6, once all three are funded, $3,292 / $2,792; the study of 2026-11-04 05:27 with the live presets).\n\nThe accounts trade the same signals, so their results are 83-88% correlated. The 6 months lose money in 3.1% (Jan-Jun) and 5.2% (Jul-Oct) of the paths. Losing all three funded accounts is rare (0.1%), because the funded sizing shrinks with the room. On 2021-25 (reference) every plan and every combination loses money.\n\nA Tradeify account is ruled out because Tradeify bans running the same bot at another firm. Apex, Take Profit Trader, Alpha and Elite ban full automation or NinjaTrader.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT on the 1-minute chart.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-1m-management.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT on the 1-minute chart\n\n> The owner asked for 1-minute entries and loss management that thinks like a person.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nOn the exact live LIT signals (2026, true 1m bars, eval sizing $800 up to a 25-pt stop + the 60% room cap), 153 variants were tested, picked on Jan-Jun and checked on Jul-Oct. 1m entry filters (inner 1m inducement, 1m displacement, volume, absorption, patience, pullback entries) all lose money in both halves.\n\nThe classic \"trade is wrong\" signals (1m CISD against, a new 1m low, back inside the swept level, higher low taken, a volume surge against) do cut 50-100% of the losers early, but they also kill 30-80% of the winners.\n\nIn Jul-Oct the winners dip as deep as the losers before they run (median 0.54R against), so every one of these signals loses money there. One signal is real in both halves: ES not confirming.\n\nWhen ES closes a 5m bar beyond its own sweep extreme and NQ then prints a 1m CISD against, before the trade has reached +0.75R, exit at the next 1m open. It cut 8 of 53 losers at about half a loss and touched no winner: H1 +$446, H2 +$1,514. That is small and rests on 8 trades.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[Displacement|displacement]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT tight stops and bigger size.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-tight-stops-size.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT tight stops and bigger size\n\n> On the exact live LIT signals (120 trades in 2026), 48 stop and entry variants were tested, each at 12 sizes, with the MFFU 50K eval simulated.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nTighter stops do not help. The 1m protected-swing stop (median 14-17 pts instead of 44-52) looks great in Jan-Jun only because the size rule then risks about 2.7 times more money.\n\nAt the same $ a trade it wins Jan-Jun and loses Jul-Oct: winners win less often (23% to 13%) because many turn into breakeven scratches, and 2021-25 loses money. Skipping wide stops leaves 0.1-1.2 trades a week. Limit entries on the 1m retest miss the best winners.\n\nThe speed has to come from size on the live sweep-extreme stop, with a cap that never risks more than 60% of the room to the max-loss floor. $800 up to a 40-pt stop + that cap passes 47% of evals within 2 weeks in both halves (live 29% / 23%), with 5% failing within 2 weeks.\n\n$800 up to a 25-pt stop + the cap keeps the live pass rate (92% / 96%) and passes faster (2-week pass 37.5% / 29.5%). 1 NQ fixed fails 28-38% of evals within 2 weeks. Paper only.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT trade selection.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-trade-selection.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT trade selection\n\n> Every LIT setup of 2026 was collected (330 setups on 167 days, 176 that would have filled if taken alone, against the 120 the bot takes) with 50 features known at the decision moment (look-ahead test passed).\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-18.\n\nPerson-like rules (an A/B/C grade, wait for a better setup, size by grade) and a small walk-forward model were scored on the real one-trade-a-day path at the eval size ($800 up to 25 pts, 60% room cap).\n\nThe model has no skill out of sample (test AUC 0.23-0.52), and the data-mined grade only works on the half it was picked on. Two simple size tilts help both halves a little. (1) Fresh trap: 1.25x when the inducement swing is at most 6 bars (30 min) old, else 0.75x.\n\nH1 +$6.4k, H2 +$0.9k, max DD lower in both halves, 2-week eval pass H1 43.0% vs 37.5%. (2) The trader's A/B/C grade, A 1.5x / C 0.5x: H1 +$1.4k, H2 +$1.6k, 2-week pass H2 34.6% vs 29.5%. Each passes a shuffle test in one half only, and 2021-25 is worse for both, so neither is proven.\n\nSkipping setups (\"wait\") never pays: a later setup rarely comes. Win rate cannot be raised without dropping below 2 trades a week. Paper only.\n\nWords used here: [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT two-week eval - how to pass the MFFU 50K in 10 sessions or less.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-two-week-eval.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT two-week eval - how to pass the MFFU 50K in 10 sessions or less\n\n> The owner's order is that an MFFU eval must pass within 2 weeks (10 sessions), so the eval mode was rebuilt for that one number, using the exact live LIT trades (same signals, same 1m entries, same stops).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nThe new idea is \"one winner passes\" sizing. Each trade is sized so that, if it reaches its target, it covers what is still missing to +$3,000. The caps are the room to the max-loss floor, the $1,000 soft daily loss and 40 micros.\n\nFixed $ schemes, a clock press, profit protection, more LIT shots a day, an earlier window and start-timing rules were tested next to it. Today's eval mode passes 40% of Jan-Jun starts and 25% of Jul-Oct starts within 10 sessions (cost per 2-week pass $228 / $373).\n\nThe recommended config (one winner passes, all the room, weekly stop on) passes 58% / 54% (Monte Carlo 51% / 57%), so a 2-week pass costs $158-171 at $91.80. The price is that 18-22% of evals fail within 2 weeks instead of 0-3%.\n\nA safer option (60% of the room, no weekly stop) passes 53% / 51% with only 3-8% failing ($174-181). No config gets past about 55-59%. LIT's ceiling is about 70%: that is the share of 10-session windows where at least one target is hit and a perfect hindsight sizing could pass.\n\nWords used here: [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/LIT untested entry ideas.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-untested-entries.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT untested entry ideas\n\n> Four entry ideas nobody had tested on LIT were run on the exact live LIT signals with the live eval sizing, 33 variants, picked on Jan-Jun 2026 and checked on Jul-Oct 2026.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nThree are rejected - a buy-stop above the 1m CISD candle (H2 -$4.8k), chasing a missed limit on a 1m pullback (H1 -$4.6k), and ES SMT as a size boost, a filter or an early 1m trigger (H2 -$0.6k to -$11.6k).\n\nOne passes the rule, barely - the LIT opening model on the overnight high/low (a 09:30-10:00 sweep of the 18:00-09:30 range, inducement taken first, 1m CISD, entry by 10:00 as an extra setup on days the bot has not traded yet).\n\nIt adds +$1,589 in Jan-Jun and +$839 in Jul-Oct at 3.4 / 3.0 trades a week and keeps the eval pass rate about the same (91% / 95% vs 92% / 96%).\n\nIt rests on only 13 trades in 2026 (3 winners), and letting the entry run to 10:15 instead of 10:00 turns Jan-Jun negative (-$4.0k), so it is a small, fragile adopt-candidate - default off, paper shadow first. Paper only.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Leakage check - does the backtest peek at the future.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/leakage-check.md\"]\n---\n\n# Leakage check - does the backtest peek at the future\n\n> No leak. Switching off the trade memory (memoryseed) and the exit learner (exitlearn) gives exactly the same 768 trades as the live strategy, so every result in this research is leak-free as it stands.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Loss anatomy - where the money leaks.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/loss-anatomy.md\"]\n---\n\n# Loss anatomy - where the money leaks\n\n> In 2021-25 the losses gave back 92% of what the winners made (285R won, 287R lost).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe leaks are long cold streaks, not big single losses (the worst day is -$875). Full stop-outs are 80% of the money lost, and trades taken in a cold streak lost money in both periods.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/MFFU Builder 25K next to the 50K.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/lit-builder-25k.md\"]\n---\n\n# MFFU Builder 25K next to the 50K\n\n> The owner is buying an MFFU Builder 25K ($63) to trade with the same LIT signals as the Builder 50K eval, with each account sized on its own room.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-19.\n\nRules were re-checked on 2026-10-19 ($1,500 target, $1,000 EOD trailing max loss locking at +$100, $600 soft daily loss, 20 micros, no eval consistency rule, 1 min day, news allowed; the $600 daily loss is in the current guide, an older \"legacy\" 25K page says none).\n\nThe 50K's config C scaled to the 25K passes 55% / 51% of evals within 2 weeks (H1 / H2), as fast as the 50K, and hardly ever fails outright. Its weak spot is different. About 12-16% of 25K evals end up \"stuck\": the room falls under $100, and even 1 micro on a 40-80 pt LIT stop would breach it, so the bot stops trading.\n\nThe pick (H1) is 50% of the room instead of 60%, and safeeval at 30% after session 10. It passes 54% / 48% in 2 weeks, 79% / 77% in the end (Monte Carlo), and is dead (failed or stuck) in 10 sessions 11% / 4%.\n\nThat is $117-132 per 2-week pass and about $80 per eventual pass at $63, against $174-181 and $115-119 on the 50K. Copy trading on the same day is close to one bet: when one account passes, the other almost always does too (correlation 0.92-0.95).\n\nWords used here: [[Consistency rule|consistency rule]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/MNQ feed vs NQ backtest.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/mnq-vs-nq-feed.md\"]\n---\n\n# MNQ feed vs NQ backtest\n\n> LIT is backtested on NQ1!, the PC decides on NinjaTrader MNQ. The PC's MNQ bars (27 heartbeat commits) equal TradingView MNQ1! on 95% of bars but are within a tick of NQ1! on only 54-62%.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-26.\n\nOn 40 sessions (Aug 24 - Oct 16, 5m execution) 36% of LIT's setups exist on one feed only, 60% of trade days differ, NQ +$2,362 vs MNQ +$367.\n\nOn the whole of 2026 with MNQ-sized noise (40 draws, 1m execution) the median is $32.8k vs $43.8k, H2 $9.1k vs $9.9k (-$229 a month; -$460 with the noise negated), only ~28% of trades identical; a vendor-noise control changes nothing.\n\nBuilt OFF - signalroot NQ reads an NQ chart's LEBarFeed files for every decision, orders / stops / targets / size stay MNQ. Paper only; nothing live changed.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Managing the trade.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/management.md\"]\n---\n\n# Managing the trade\n\n> Today's management is at its sweet spot on all-days rules, with one exception: on trending days (10-day efficiency above 0.45) waiting until +3R to move to breakeven adds +$4.7k to 2021-25 (PF 1.09 to 1.15) and leaves 2026 net unchanged (+$20.1k).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nOn today's sizing it adds some late-May 2026 eval fails (beyond 1 month); with the sizing research's quarter size in cold streaks it is clean (+$4.8k in 2021-25). Profit locks, other time stops and giveback levels all lose.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]], [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Mind - a thinking layer for Zlarior, ATLAS and BLITZ (similar-setup.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/mind.md\"]\n---\n\n# Mind - a thinking layer for Zlarior, ATLAS and BLITZ (similar-setup\n\n> Can think for itself.\" engine/mind.py remembers every historical setup of each bot (631 Zlarior, 1,129 ATLAS, 330 BLITZ) with the features the live bot can compute, and for a live setup shows the nearest past setups (win %, average R, $ at today's...\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-29.\n\nTested honestly (fit on 2026 H1, checked on 2026 H2 and 2021-25), similarity does not predict the outcome for any bot (2026 H2 rank correlation of predicted vs real R: Zlarior +0.00 - the setups it rated best made 0.30R less than those it rated worst -, ATLAS -0.25, BLITZ -0.05), so the dashboards show it as...\n\nWith 5 years as memory (fit 2021-25) ATLAS similarity did separate 2026 (top third +1.7R / +1.8R vs bottom +0.5R / +0.2R, p 0.03 in each half), but the same check a year earlier (fit 2021-24, check 2025) shows nothing (+0.23R / +0.24R): not confirmed.\n\nA small logistic model gives the trend-day odds at 10:30 ET: AUC 0.66 on 2026 H2 and 0.63 on 2021-25; when it said >= 60%, 51% of 2021-25 days were trend days (base rate 33%).\n\nMind writes an if-then game plan per bot at 01:45 / 10:45 ET, reviews every closed trade (grade, right / wrong, lesson, luck or skill) and runs a nightly self-research job that tests its own ideas on 2026 H1 (pick) / H2 (check) / 2021-25: the first run noticed 9 patterns, tested 22 size / skip rules (with its...\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Momentum scalps for a third account.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/scalp-momentum.md\"]\n---\n\n# Momentum scalps for a third account\n\n> 10 momentum / breakout / continuation families (about 10,000 settings, each run on 1m and 5m bars) were tested on CME NQ1!\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-28.\n\n(1m for 2026, 5m for 2021-25), costs in ($1.90 a micro, 1 tick on every market and stop fill, limits only when traded through, every same-bar tie booked against us), picked on 2026 Jan-Jun, checked on Jul-Oct, 2021-25 on 5m as a second check.\n\nOpening-range breakouts, EMA pullbacks, CISD continuation, N-bar breakouts, ES-leads-NQ and news bars all fail the July-October check.\n\nOne family survives - the noise-area momentum scalp (a close outside the 09:30 open's normal time-of-day range, checked every 30 minutes 10:30-15:30, stop 1.5 x the 5m ATR, trail 1.5R after +1R, out after 30 minutes): 11-12 trades a week, H1 PF 1.37 (+$4,870 at $150 a trade, $799/mo), H2 PF 1.22 (+$1,729, $466/mo)...\n\nBut a 60-point stop is $122 a micro against a $1,000 trailing loss, so the 25K eval passes only 21% in 2 weeks (fails 4% H1, 19% H2) and a funded 25K pays about $0-100 a month (it gets stuck at the floor).\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[ATR]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Monte Carlo of the MFFU 50K eval.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/monte-carlo-evals.md\"]\n---\n\n# Monte Carlo of the MFFU 50K eval\n\n> One 50K eval on the locked strategy has about a 35% chance of passing within 60 sessions, 30% of failing and 35% of still running (the real history says 41 / 22 / 37); a typical pass takes 28 sessions, and a funded account costs 1.9 evals and about...\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nStarting the eval during a cold streak cuts the 1-month pass odds from 22% to 4%, so time the purchase to the filter. The fast press gets funded in a median 9.8 weeks for 3.7 months of fees but fails half of its evals.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Moving averages as confluence.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/moving-averages.md\"]\n---\n\n# Moving averages as confluence\n\n> The textbook \"trade with the EMA trend\" filters all cost money (2026 net falls by $5-8k).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe one moving-average fact that helps is a warning sign: when price is 3+ hourly ATRs on the wrong side of the 1-hour EMA 20 (a \"falling knife\"), trading at half size lifts 2021-25 from +$6.5k to +$9.6k (+$127 to +$189 a month, PF 1.09 to 1.13, 1-month eval fails 9.4% to 6.2%) while 2026 is unchanged (+$20.1k, PF...\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Night R&D (money + eval agent).md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\ndate: 2026-11-03\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-03-night-rnd.md\"]\n---\n\n# Night R&D (money + eval agent)\n\n> Three studies, each on the owner's metrics (2026 halves and 2021-25, $ a month net of every eval, MFFU 2-week pass / fail).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-11-03.\n\n(1) The eval size solved state by state (dynamic programming on Jan-Jun 2026, checked on Jul-Oct and 2021-25) - a better pass / fail frontier than any room share (2-week fails 17.8 / 20.9 / 24.9% -> 2.9 / 2.9 / 8.3% at about the same pass rate) but $18 / $34 / $3 a month less per slot and slower passes, so not adopted.\n\n(2) Where each bot should sit - a Zlarior copy on the free MFFU 25K pays $334-375 / $269-331 / -$7..-15 a month vs BLITZ's $286 / $52 / -$39; ATLAS stays on its 25K (it is worth more than a 2nd Zlarior 25K and is the only bot uncorrelated with Zlarior).\n\nWith LIT 50K copies at other firms, 6 accounts make about $3,900 a month on Jul-Oct trades. (3) Slippage - Zlarior and ATLAS survive +4 ticks a round trip; BLITZ's 2021-25 edge is gone at +2.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Parameter robustness.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/parameter-robustness.md\"]\n---\n\n# Parameter robustness\n\n> Mostly a plateau, with one ridge. Cold size, late size, the trending-day breakeven (anything from +3R to never gives the same money), the $ risk and the wide-stop rule all move smoothly.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe exception is the cold-streak lookback of 15 signals: 15 and 16 give +$16.4k / +$16.9k in 2021-25, but 14 gives +$13.3k, 13 gives +$7.8k and 17 gives +$10.7k, so about half of the 5-year gain over the morning rules (+$6.5k) depends on that one number.\n\nEvery one of 105 neighbouring combinations stays profitable in both periods (2021-25 worst +$6.8k, 2026 worst +$19.6k). The lock has 17 of 55 rolling 6-month windows negative (morning rules 22) and a longest flat stretch of 68 weeks (85).\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Retail strategies on their own (EMA, MACD, support-resistance incl. ORB.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/retail-standalone.md\"]\n---\n\n# Retail strategies on their own (EMA, MACD, support-resistance incl. ORB\n\n> The owner asked whether EMA, MACD, support/resistance and supply/demand strategies make money on their own, separate from LIT.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-22.\n\n13,320 versions (8,636 with different trades) were tested as standalone NQ strategies on real CME NQ1! 5m data, with costs ($1.40 a micro + 1 tick slippage), structural stops, picked on 2021-11..2024-12 and checked untouched on 2025 and 2026. Three families have versions that made money in all three periods.\n\nThe best robust ones: S/R bounce at the prior-day / overnight high and low (+$40.3k / +$36.1k / +$6.1k at $500 risk a trade, PF 1.20 / 1.79 / 1.16), the 8/21 EMA opening-hour momentum (+$37.9k / +$23.8k / +$5.4k, PF 1.11 / 1.25 / 1.07) and the 15m EMA ribbon (+$41.6k / +$12.6k / +$2.5k).\n\nPD/ON break-and-retest with a limit at the level is the strongest in 2026 (PF 3.13, +$21.2k) but was flat in 2025. MACD versions picked in-sample are flat to negative in 2026. Supply/demand loses in almost every version.\n\nAll edges are thin next to LIT (2026 PF 1.1-1.2 vs 4.07), 2026 H2 is negative for most, and the eval odds are far worse than LIT's. Correlation with LIT is low (-0.1 to +0.26). Worth paper-trading the PD/ON bounce and the opening-hour EMA as a second-account idea; not worth real money yet.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Risk and sizing - every sizing idea tested on both periods.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/risk-sizing.md\"]\n---\n\n# Risk and sizing - every sizing idea tested on both periods\n\n> Changing how much the bot risks per trade moves money and drawdown together and does not fix 2021-25.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nMore risk ($1,000-1,200, or no wide-stop scaling) makes more in 2026 but doubles eval fails; less risk drops below 2 setups a week. The changes that help both periods size down specific losing trades: quarter size in cold streaks, half size on late entries.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Risk summary - size down the two kinds of trades that lose.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/risk-summary.md\"]\n---\n\n# Risk summary - size down the two kinds of trades that lose\n\n> Recommendation: quarter size (not half) in cold streaks and half size on late entries.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-17.\n\nSame trades: 2021-25 +$11.5k instead of +$6.5k (PF 1.19 vs 1.09, max DD -$9.2k vs -$11.4k), 2026 +$20.6k instead of +$20.1k (PF 4.12 vs 3.48), 1-month eval fails in 2021-25 9.4% to 5.1%.\n\nFor the 2-week eval rule, an optional fast mode ($800 on any stop until the eval is $1,000 down) doubles eval speed at the same evals per pass but fails more evals a year.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/STOP-HUNT audit - trying to break the Asia resweep hunt and the trend.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/stophunt-audit.md\"]\n---\n\n# STOP-HUNT audit - trying to break the Asia resweep hunt and the trend\n\n> An adversarial check of the two stop-hunt leads (stophunt.md) before anyone trusts them, 7 checks.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-23.\n\nThe code is honest. An independent rewrite reproduces every trade (558/558 Asia, 467/467 trend, every field), there is no look-ahead (30/30 trades per lead re-derived on data cut at the signal), and costs hurt only a little (+1 tick plus 1 extra night tick keeps 78-99% of the money).\n\nBoth leads are positive in every year 2022-2026 and in 95-98% of the one-step neighbours. The proof is weak. The leads are NOT the pick rule's own top choices: the trend hunt was number 10 of its 12-pick shortlist and had the best H2 of the 12; the Asia hunt was number 9 and had the 2nd best H2.\n\nSo 2026 H2 was not really untouched. On H2 alone the Asia hunt is not significant (t 0.8, p 0.21, 3 best trades = all its H2 money). The trend hunt looks significant (p 0.03-0.05), but that drops to p 0.3-0.4 once you allow for picking the best of 12. Deflated Sharpe is about 0 for both against the size of the search.\n\nTrue out-of-sample test - the researcher's own search re-run on 2021-11..2024-12 only. The generic grid's picks lose in 2025 and 2026. Inside the trend family every pre-2025 shortlist pick loses in 2025 (0/12) and in H2 (0/12), and the trend lead itself would not even have been eligible (2024 PF 1.11).\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/STOP-HUNT family - retail stop pools, the hunt, a resweep or inducement.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/stophunt.md\"]\n---\n\n# STOP-HUNT family - retail stop pools, the hunt, a resweep or inducement\n\n> Built as its own strategy, any hour 24/5 - 16 retail pool types (1m/5m/15m/1h swings, equal highs/lows, range edges, 3-touch trendlines, Asia/London/NY/overnight/day/week highs and lows, round numbers) ranked by obviousness, 9 hunt variants (immediate...\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-23.\n\n28.7 million variants on CME NQ1! (costs $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, $500 risk a trade, no look-ahead, checked on data cut at each signal).\n\nThe honest headline - on average a stop-hunt reversal is worth nothing (about 0 R before costs, win 46-49%), and a period's result says nothing about another period (t-stat correlation H1 vs H2 -0.003).\n\nPicking on 2026 H1 alone, as asked, fails - the H1 winners (80% win rate) lose in 2021-24 and are positive in H2 no more often than chance. Picking on H1 + 2025 + 2021-24 with H2 untouched finds two leads, both with a win rate of 55% or more in every period.\n\n(1) Asia resweep hunt (18:00-02:00, 15m/1h/session pools, re-run, 1.2 ATR candle beyond the last swing, 2R) - H1 +$8.1k (71% win, PF 2.60), H2 +$2.9k (58%, PF 1.37), 2025 +$11.0k, 2021-24 +$22.7k, about 2 trades a week; random-time placebo p 0.000 over all periods but 0.25 in H2 alone; this region of settings beats...\n\nWords used here: [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]], [[ATR]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Second strategies on LIT's account.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-route.md\"]\n---\n\n# Second strategies on LIT's account\n\n> The PC bot can now trade the second strategies (EMA London pullback, MACD sweep, S/R NY reclaim) on LIT's own NinjaTrader account next to LIT, one switch per strategy, shipped OFF.\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-22.\n\nAccount rules - one position at a time, LIT first (no second entry while LIT is in a trade, entering, has a limit armed or waits for its 1m CISD; an open second trade is closed at market when LIT enters), max 2 trades a day with one slot always LIT's, eval-safe size ($500 capped at 60% of the room and the shared...\n\nUnder these rules the EMA's eval-path gain shrinks a lot - H2 path +$11.2k instead of the research's +$23.0k (LIT alone +$9.1k), H2 2-week pass/fail 69.6/0.0 (research 69.6/2.9, LIT alone 52.2/0.0) - and H1 gets worse than LIT alone (2-week fail 10.9% vs 5.9%).\n\nThe main cost is the owner's \"no entry while LIT is armed\" rule (17 of 71 EMA trades, incl. 4 targets); the LIT-priority close adds the H1 fails. Keep the switch off on the eval; second-mix.md's two-account plan stays the better setup.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Second strategy next to LIT (learned).md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-md.md\"]\n---\n\n# Second strategy next to LIT (learned)\n\n> 221,184 versions of two families were tested on CME NQ1! (5m, 1m trade path in 2026), costs $1.90 a micro + 1 tick, structural stops, picked on 2026 H1 only and checked untouched on H2.\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-22.\n\nMACD divergence at a LIT liquidity sweep with a 5m CISD (London 02:00-05:00 + NY 09:30-11:00, VIX-band target banked at 8R, LIT's 15-minute check) is positive in all four periods at $500 risk - H1 +$22,087 (PF 3.78), H2 +$2,569 (PF 1.75), 2025 +$8,183, 2021-24 +$11,982 - at about 1.1 trades a week, daily...\n\nPicking on H1 did predict H2 better than chance for this family (top H1 versions 66-70% positive in H2 vs 34% for all). But it lives on a few big winners (H2 without its best 3 trades is -5.5R) and the 2021-25 drawdown is large (-$22.6k at $500).\n\nNext to LIT on one account (LIT first, max 2 trades a day) it adds +$14.1k in H1 and +$1.6k in H2 and lifts 2-week eval passes. Supply/demand - no version holds: the H1 picks lose in H2 (worse than chance). Verdict - paper-trade the MACD sweep next to LIT, no real money yet.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Second strategy next to LIT.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/second-ema.md\"]\n---\n\n# Second strategy next to LIT\n\n> 192,776 versions of an EMA trend pullback with LIT's tools (sweep, 5m CISD, VIX-band target, BE + costs, 15-minute check) were tested on CME NQ1!\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-10-22.\n\n5m bars, with costs ($1.90 a micro + 1 tick). Parameters were picked on 2026 H1 and checked untouched on H2. The best version trades London hours (03:00-09:30 ET) when the 15m and 1h EMA 21 are above the 50, after a pullback to the 5m EMA 50 that sweeps a swing low and then closes back up (CISD).\n\nStop 1 tick beyond the sweep wick, target = LIT's VIX band, BE + 1.25 at 1R. At $500 risk a trade it made H1 +$19.8k (PF 2.72, $3,280/mo) and H2 +$8.1k (PF 3.10, $2,191/mo) at about 2 trades a week, and it also stayed positive in 2025 (+$6.2k, PF 1.18) and 2021-24 (+$5.5k, PF 1.05). Daily correlation with LIT is 0.19.\n\nOn LIT's account (LIT first, max 2 trades a day) 2026 goes from +$43.4k to +$66.8k, with max DD -$3.5k instead of -$2.2k. On the eval path the 2-week pass/fail goes from 57.1/5.9 to 61.3/5.0 in H1 and from 52.2/0.0 to 69.6/2.9 in H2. All 13 one-step neighbours are positive in H1 and H2.\n\nVerdict - paper-trade it next to LIT; not live until it shows on paper.\n\nWords used here: [[CISD]], [[VIX band]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Micro|micro]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Sizing cap invariant.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/sizing-caps.md\"]\n---\n\n# Sizing cap invariant\n\n> Pre-launch audit.\n\nAdopted, 2026-10-24.\n\nBefore, every cap (60% of the eval room, the soft daily loss, the room caps of eval / safeeval, account B's $150 / $300 safe size, ATLAS's $500, the extra accounts' daily loss) rounded a too-small result back UP to 1 micro, so a wide stop or an almost-used budget could still send 1 micro whose stop-out broke the...\n\nNow the micros are computed after all caps; if not even 1 micro fits, the entry is skipped. Every cap prices 1 micro at (stop + 1 tick) x $2 + $1.90. A final check (sizing.ceilings) runs last in every eval mode, incl. the 1-micro minimum-days trade - room, soft daily loss and (when switched on) the weekly loss budget.\n\nMoney on the 2026 eval path: fresh-eval 2-week pass unchanged (LIT 57.1 / 52.2), 2-week fails 5.9 / 0.0 -> 0.0 / 0.0, but the drained evals no longer die - they get \"stuck\" (room too small for almost any entry).\n\nWith the old path rule (new eval only after a pass / fail) a stuck eval sits forever: H1 +$34.3k -> +$27.2k, H2 +$9.1k -> -$1.9k. With a stuck rule (room < $200 = buy a new eval) both versions match: H1 +$27.9k -> +$28.5k, H2 +$15.3k -> +$14.8k.\n\nWords used here: [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/The eval game - which plan, when to start, two accounts, after a fail.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/eval-game.md\"]\n---\n\n# The eval game - which plan, when to start, two accounts, after a fail\n\n> The MFFU Builder 50K is the right eval for this strategy (1.24 evals per pass in 2026, 2.52 in 2021-25, about $313 / $1,006 of fees per funded account); the $1.5k add-on fails twice as often, plans with a best-day rule in the eval take 2-17 months...\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nA second account on the same trades buys no speed, after a fail the next pass takes a median 3.3 months, and once funded the first payout takes 3.3 (2026) / 7.7 (2021-25) months if MFFU's 50% best-day rule applies at payout (1.3 / 1.7 months if not) while 21% / 58% of funded accounts hit the trailing loss before...\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/The funded path - from a passed eval to money in the bank.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/funded-path.md\"]\n---\n\n# The funded path - from a passed eval to money in the bank\n\n> Once an MFFU Builder eval passes, the funded account is where the money is.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-25.\n\nReplayed on the 2026 trades (costs in, $1.90 a micro) through the new sim funded tracker (engine/fundedlife.py) and the research funded sizing, a funded Builder 50K trading Zlarior (LIT) pays you about $835 a month on the Jul-Oct check half ($889 Jan-Jun), loses 0% of accounts in 6 months, and its first payout...\n\nATLAS on a funded 25K pays $318 / $622 a month but loses 13.6% of accounts in 6 months on Jul-Oct. Zlarior on a second 25K pays $389 / $329. All three together: about $1,540 a month (Jul-Oct) to $1,840 (Jan-Jun) for $217.80 of evals.\n\nA consistency-aware size (shrink a day's winner to stay under the 50% rule) was built and tested: it cuts the days the money waits on the 50% rule from 37 to 13 but pays $76 a month less on Jan-Jun, so it stays OFF.\n\nNothing live changed: a passed eval still stops and tells the owner; funded trading starts only with the owner's command.\n\nWords used here: [[Consistency rule|consistency rule]], [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/The money plan on today's settings (night).md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\ndate: 2026-11-03\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-03-money-plan.md\"]\n---\n\n# The money plan on today's settings (night)\n\n> MFFU lets one person hold 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25Ks.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-11-03.\n\nToday - Zlarior (LIT) on the 50K, ATLAS on one 25K, the other 25K (BLITZ's) off. Every slot was priced the same way - 6 months of eval -> 2 sessions of paperwork -> funded -> MFFU payouts -> the next eval, net of every eval, 1,000 Monte Carlo paths of the real sessions, live eval and funded sizing, $1.90 a micro.\n\nPer month (H1 Jan-Jun 2026 / H2 Jul-Oct 2026 / 2021-25 reference) - LIT 50K $823 / $798 / -$7; ATLAS 25K $568 / $382 / -$2; LIT 25K $375 / $331 / -$15 (room 0.8) or $334 / $269 / -$7 (the fleet's 25K preset, 50% room); BLITZ 25K $286 / $52 / -$39.\n\nSo the free 25K earns most as a Zlarior copy - better than BLITZ in all three spans. The three bots' daily results are uncorrelated (LIT-ATLAS -0.01 / -0.03 / +0.11), while Zlarior copies move together (0.8-0.9).\n\nBeyond MFFU every extra LIT 50K-type account adds about $790 a month in a 2026-like market and costs about $25 a month (its evals) in a 2021-25-like one.\n\nWords used here: [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/The scaling plan - from 2 accounts (LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K) to 10, when.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/scale-plan.md\"]\n---\n\n# The scaling plan - from 2 accounts (LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K) to 10, when\n\n> And keep scaling.\" A ladder of five steps (2 / 4 / 6 / 8 / 10 accounts, two accounts a week apart per step) was modelled with the stored studies' own executors (LIT - tools/litfundedcombo.py on the live LIT trades, scale-in off; ATLAS...\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-22.\n\nNext accounts - LucidPro 50K and FundedNext Rapid Daily 50K (bots and copying your own accounts allowed); MFFU holds LIT once (Builder 50K) and ATLAS once (Builder 25K).\n\nEach step waits for a payout trigger (first payout; then 3 funded + $2,000 paid; 4 funded + $5,000; 6 funded + $10,000) and for cash (a $300 budget + payouts - fees).\n\nOn Jul-Oct 2026 trades (the half to quote) the steps pay about $1,160 / $2,850 / $4,600 / $6,100 / $7,900 a month once held, reached in a median 2.1 / 3.6 / 5.1 / 6.5 months for about $440 / $670 / $1,010 / $1,410 of evals.\n\nOn 2025 trades the same steps pay $90-560 a month and the payout triggers stop the ladder by themselves - only 13% of 2025-like paths reach 10 accounts in 24 months, and the 24-month result is about break-even (median +$100, 1 in 10 -$1,660).\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Trade construction.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/trade-construction-summary.md\"]\n---\n\n# Trade construction\n\n> One change is worth making, together with the sizing change: on trending days move the stop to breakeven at +3R instead of +1R.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-17.\n\nWith the sizing change it adds +$4.8k to 2021-25 (PF 1.19 to 1.27) and changes nothing in 2026 evals; on today's sizing alone it causes extra late-May 2026 eval fails, so then keep the current rules. Stops, targets, the other exits, filters and timing are already at their best; 120+ variants tested, no leak found.\n\nWords used here: [[Breakeven|breakeven]], [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Uprisk - where the funded money is capped, and what really pays more.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/uprisk.md\"]\n---\n\n# Uprisk - where the funded money is capped, and what really pays more\n\n> On the 2026 LIT trades a funded MFFU Builder 50K already makes about $1,950 a month of trading profit, but only about $1,050 a month comes out ($835-889 to you).\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-25.\n\nRisk per trade is not what holds it back. MFFU pays at most $2,000 a request, the 50% best-day rule holds the money about 38 of 126 sessions, and after 5 payouts ($8,000 to you) the account leaves the sim. So after 6 months about $5,400 is still sitting in the account.\n\nDoubling the per-trade risk from $800 to $1,500 adds only $3-62 a month. The one sizing lever that pays is the winner cap, the cap that keeps a day's best win small for the 50% rule.\n\nRaising it from $1,500 to $2,000 (one winner = one full request), with 60% of the room and at most $1,500 a trade, pays $929 / $915 a month instead of $889 / $835 (H1 pick / H2 check). The first payout comes after 23-26 sessions instead of 33, and 0% of accounts are lost.\n\nA more aggressive eval (roomf 0.8, weekly loss stop off) adds about $20-30 a month per account and fails about 1 eval in 5 within 2 weeks (today about 1 in 35). The real multiplier is the number of accounts, because each one pays about the same and they add up.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/VWAP and volume profile as confluence.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/vwap-volume-profile.md\"]\n---\n\n# VWAP and volume profile as confluence\n\n> None of 24 VWAP and volume-profile ideas made the live strategy better in both 2026 and 2021-25 when used to size trades up or down (every trade still taken, 2.2 and 3.2 trades a week).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nThe only raw pass, 1.25x size on days that open inside yesterday's value area (2026 PF 3.50 vs 3.48, 2021-25 PF 1.10 vs 1.09), fails the robustness checks, so the live strategy stays as it is.\n\nWords used here: [[PF (profit factor)]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/What to expect - streaks, drawdowns, flat months and the odds inside.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/what-to-expect.md\"]\n---\n\n# What to expect - streaks, drawdowns, flat months and the odds inside\n\n> The locked strategy wins 1 trade in 4-5 and loses or scratches the rest, so long runs without a winner are normal: 33 trades in a row without a win happened once (Sep-Nov 2025, -$3,126), and 2026 had 16 (Apr-May, -$1,667).\n\nA finding, nothing changed, 2026-10-17.\n\nA loss does not make the next trade worse (after a loss +0.02R, after a win -0.01R in 2021-25, p=0.79), so sitting out or halving the trade after a max-size loss loses money in both periods (rejected).\n\nMost months lose a little (median month -$184 over 5 years, 40% of months positive; 2026 median +$1,162, 50% positive); 13 trades made the whole 5-year profit. In a 2-week eval expect 4+ non-winners in a row in about half of the starts (2021-25 58%, 2026 27%), a $1,000 dip in 12% (2026 1%), a $2,000 dip in 1%.\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Win rate and more trades.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/win-rate-and-sessions.md\"]\n---\n\n# Win rate and more trades\n\n> More trades from new windows or second trades lose money in 2021-25 every time (London, Asia, pre-open, lunch, late PM, 2 a day), even at a quarter size.\n\nWorth adopting (the owner decides), 2026-10-18.\n\nThe things that raise the win rate (partials, a depth limit, HTF filters) earn less money or cut trades below 2 a week.\n\nOne small change helps both periods with every trade kept: half size when the sweep ran 3.5+ ATR past the pool (2026 +$293, PF 3.94 to 4.11; 2021-25 +$1.5k, PF 1.27 to 1.32, max drawdown -$9.3k to -$7.5k, 1-month eval fails 5.2% to 3.5%).\n\nWords used here: [[ATR]], [[PF (profit factor)]], [[Sweep|sweep]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/What we learned/Zlarior eval size worked out state by state (dynamic programming,.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, learned]\ndate: 2026-11-03\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-03-eval-dp.md\"]\n---\n\n# Zlarior eval size worked out state by state (dynamic programming,\n\n> The live eval size is one rule for every eval state (micros so one winner covers what is left to +$3,000, capped at 80% of the room).\n\nA promising lead, paper first, 2026-11-03.\n\nTonight the size was solved for EACH state - balance, where the trailing floor sits, sessions left of the 10 - by backward induction on the 128 real Jan-Jun 2026 LIT sessions, choosing among 28 sizes (0.5-3x the one-winner size, 40-100% room caps) to maximise P(pass in 10) minus lam x P(fail in 10).\n\nThe frozen tables were played through the real eval code on Jul-Oct 2026 and 2021-25.\n\nThe lam 0.5 table sits on a better pass / fail frontier than every room-share setting (at the old safe set's fail rate it passes 2-4 points more evals in all three spans), and on the real 2026 order it cuts 2-week fails from 16-23% to 1-5% with the same pass rate.\n\nBut the owner's money metric (the whole slot: eval -> funded -> payouts) is $18 / $34 / $3 a month lower than live, and passes take longer. The owner chose speed and money on 2026-10-25 (room 0.8, \"evals: one hit, make it quick\"), so nothing changes.\n\nWords used here: [[Micro|micro]].\n\n---\nUp: [[What we learned]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/ATLAS research - account B on the Builder 25K.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, second-strategies]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/atlas.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATLAS research - account B on the Builder 25K\n\n> adopt-candidate (unchanged rules; built, OFF; paper / Sim first)\n\n**Full title:** ATLAS - account B (EMA + MACD + S/R) tuned as a whole for the Builder 25K (nothing beats today; kept as is)\n\n## Summary\nOwner (2026-10-22) - LIT on the 50K eval, the new strategy now called ATLAS (account B - London EMA pullback + MACD sweep divergence + S/R NY reclaim) on a Builder 25K; \"keep tweaking this strategy, make it ready to trade my other account\". ATLAS was scored under the REAL account-B rules, now including the one the research lacked (no ATLAS entry while LIT has an ARMED setup the other way). With it, today's ATLAS on a Builder 25K passes 72.3% of evals within 2 weeks in 2026 H1 (0.0% fail, 5.0% stuck) and 43.5% in H2 (0.0 / 0.0), eval-path +$19,737 ($3,238/mo) and +$5,448 ($1,467/mo), about $1,840 paid out in the first 3 funded months (H1 starts), +$1,549 per $63 eval bought. 358 tweaks were tested (members, Asia slot, per-member size weights down AND up, priority, 25K sizing incl. room 40-60% / safe / hybrid / daily cap, entry windows, weekdays, VIX filters and VIX size, per-member...\n\n## The answer in one table\n| Family (tweaks) | Better in H1 | Better in both | By chance | Best H1 tweak: H1 / H2 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Today** |  |  |  | **72.3 / 5.0, +$19,737 · 43.5 / 0.0, +$5,448** |\n| Members, incl. the Asia slot (14) | 0 | 0 | 0 | + Asia: 70.6 / 9.2, +$19,378 · 58.0 / 0.0, +$7,624 |\n| Size weights per member (26) | 0 | 0 | 0 | MACD 0.75: 65.5 / 5.0, +$17,187 · 43.5 / 0.0, +$5,554 |\n| Priority on the same bar (5) | 0 | 0 | 0 | all equal to today in H1 |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/atlas.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Second strategies]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/BLITZ research - its own Builder 25K.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, second-strategies]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/blitz-25k.md\"]\n---\n\n# BLITZ research - its own Builder 25K\n\n> no adopt-candidate - no BLITZ rule change holds in 2026 H1, H2 and 2021-25 (0 of 1,997 variants); verdict no-change (research/scalp/blitz_25k_pick.json). Only the 25K eval size for its own account changes (40% of the room, then 100% from session 5): about 72% of evals decided in 2 weeks instead of 40%, same money. The 25K slot still loses about $40 a month in 2021-25 with every setting. Paper only, nothing switched.\n\n**Full title:** BLITZ on its own MFFU Builder 25K (account C) - keep today's rule; trading \"like a person\" (structure stops, liquidity targets, swing trails...\n\n## Summary\nOwner 2026-10-28/29 - BLITZ gets its own MFFU Builder 25K next week; make it trade \"like a person, not a fixed rr\" and \"learn market structure\", judged on money and eval speed. Every variant ran as account C (never opposite LIT or ATLAS, closed first at their opposite entry), 2026 on 1m bars (pick on Jan-Jun, check on Jul-Oct 16), 2021-25 the same rule on 5m bars, costs in ($1.90 a micro, 1 tick on market / stop fills, same-bar tie = loss). The anti-hedge rule costs nothing in 2026 (+$51 / +$90 a month at $150 a trade; of 330 trades 12 skipped, 5 added, 1 closed early) and $16 a month in 2021-25. Structure stops (45: last 1m / 5m swing, the move's low, min / max caps) make less than today in H1 and 2021-25 (most lose in 2021-25). Targets at the next liquidity, partials at the first level, swing trails, change-of-character exits and no clock (1,920 variants on 5 stops): 18 beat today in...\n\n## The answer in one table\n| Span | Trades | / week | Weeks with 2+ | Win % | Avg R | PF | Net | **$ / month** | Max DD |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 2026 H1 (pick) | 217 | 8.5 | 89% | 56.7 | +0.165 | 1.70 | +$5,873 | **+$963** | -$1,152 |\n| **2026 H2 (check)** | 106 | 6.8 | 75% | 56.6 | +0.200 | **1.62** | **+$2,478** | **+$667** | -$815 |\n| 2021-25 (5m) | 1,841 | 8.6 | 80% | 50.1 | +0.013 | 1.05 | +$3,765 | +$73 | -$5,512 |\n| same rule alone (no anti-hedge rule) H1 / H2 / 2021-25 | 221 / 109 / 1,869 |  |  |  | +0.150 / +0.171 / +0.016 | 1.63 / 1.49 / 1.06 |  | +$912 / +$577 / +$89 |  |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/blitz-25k.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Second strategies]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/Kalshi edges - why it loses and what might work.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, kalshi]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md\"]\n---\n\n# Kalshi edges - why it loses and what might work\n\n> Kalshi Edge v2: why it loses, and what (if anything) works\n\n## Summary\n2026-11-04. Research only. No Kalshi API call, no order, no secret, the bot untouched. Data: the public feed kalshi.json (1,836 settled buys with price/asset/strategy totals, 41 days of daily P&L, the last 40 trades). Numbers regenerate with python tools/kalshi_research.py (tables in research/kalshi/edge_tables.json, schema in the script's docstring). Everything below is **after Kalshi's fees**: the bot's own P&L already has the fee taken out (checked on the last 40 trades: fee = 0.07 x contracts x price x (1 - price), to the cent).\n\n## The answer in one table\n| Question | Answer |\n| --- | --- |\n| Why is it negative? | Kalshi's fee. Before fees the bot is at about +$10 on $21,602 bet (0.05%); the fees are about $73 (0.34% of the money bet). It buys fairly priced favourites and pays the toll. |\n| Is there a positive pocket? | Maybe one: entries at **95c and above**. 1,075 trades, 98.0% won (97.0% to 98.7%) against 96.9% needed. +$206 on $15,275 (+1.4% per $, range +0.5% to +2.1%). Thin, and it is the pay for tail risk... |\n| What loses? | **90-95c entries: -$248** (581 trades, ROI -4.7%, range -7.9% to -1.6%). **XRP: -$107** (192 trades, ROI -6.1%). **15-min favourites: -$89** (95.0% won, needs 96.6%; already OFF). Under 90c: -$20... |\n| Why a 94% win rate still loses | A win pays about **$0.71**, a loss costs about **$12.89**: one loss erases 18 wins on average (50+ wins at 98c). Break-even loss rate is 5.2%, the bot's is 5.5%. |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/2026-11-04_kalshi_edges.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Kalshi bot research]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/LIT ideas night - 16 new ideas tested.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, lit]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_lit_ideas_night.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT ideas night - 16 new ideas tested\n\n> nothing to switch on today; one size tilt (stop tight in ATR) beats its shuffles in both halves and is the only candidate worth a forward paper check; three thin leads; everything else rejected\n\n**Full title:** LIT ideas night - 16 new ideas (165 versions) on the live LIT - partials and stair stops, a second cut check, news, VIX / time / weekday / price-place /...\n\n## Summary\nJob 26. 16 new ideas for Zlarior (live LIT) written down with a reason, then run as 165 versions on the exact live trades (the re-walk equals all 631 live trades - every exit, point and micro), CME NQ futures, true 1m bars in 2026, costs in. Picked on Jan-Jun 2026, checked on Jul-Oct 2026, 2021-25 as a reference only. Money three ways - LIT size rule, the real MFFU 50K eval path (today's two_week sizing) and the funded 50K. No management change (partials, stair stops, stale exits, news exits, a later cut) and no entry-window or per-session change holds in both halves. One size idea survives - a bigger size when the stop is tight against the 5m ATR.\n\n## The answer in one table\n| What | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| What I did | 16 new ideas for Zlarior, run as **165 versions** on today's live trades (real CME NQ, true 1-minute bars in 2026, costs in). Picked on Jan-Jun 2026, checked on Jul-Oct 2026. |\n| Today's bar to beat | Jan-Jun **+$33,901 ($5,562/mo, PF 4.98)**, Jul-Oct **+$9,919 ($2,670/mo, PF 2.91)**, 3.1 / 2.6 trades a week. Eval 2-week pass / fail **57.1 / 1.7%** and **52.2 / 5.8%**. |\n| Change the live bot? | **No change tonight.** Nothing was switched on or off. |\n| Partial exits (1/3 or 1/2 out at 1R, 2R, 3R, 4R), stair stops | **Worse.** Jan-Jun -$4.5k to -$17k and Jul-Oct down too; they only look good in the 2021-25 line, which is not a gate. |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/2026-11-04_lit_ideas_night.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Rejected ideas]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/LIT ideas round 2 - scale-in and six more ideas.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, lit]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_lit_ideas_round2.md\"]\n---\n\n# LIT ideas round 2 - scale-in and six more ideas\n\n> nothing to switch on; scale-in is more robust than the \"three trades\" read suggested (still needs your Sim roundtrip); 27 new versions, none passes the gate; two leads (few-touches size, level-reuse size) are not strong enough; Plutus rebuilt from the live-settings inputs\n\n**Full title:** LIT ideas round 2 - scale-in again (bootstrap by session, best trades removed in steps) and six more new ideas (Asia-slot result, level age, level touches...\n\n## Summary\nJob 28. (1) Scale-in at LIT size re-measured with a bootstrap over sessions and over weeks and with only the best 1, 2 or 3 trades removed. (2) Six new LIT ideas, 27 versions on the exact live trades (the re-walk equals all 631 live trades), judged Jan-Jun pick / Jul-Oct check, costs in, size tilts shuffle-tested. (3) Plutus (plans.json) rebuilt from the live-settings research files instead of the 10-19 study.\n\n## The answer in one table\n| What | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Plutus numbers | **Fixed, rebuilt from the research files** (not hand-edited): Builder 50K funded **$929 / $915** a month, live 2-week eval pass / fail **52.9 / 17.8** and **58.6 / 20.9**, first payout shown **both... |\n| Scale-in (still OFF), again | The gain is **not just three lucky trades**. Jan-Jun +$10,621 (35 trades), Jul-Oct +$5,344 (18). Bootstrap by session: positive in **93% / 99%** of resamples; by week **97% / 100%**. Remove the... |\n| 6 new LIT ideas (27 versions) | **None passes.** Asia-slot result: only 11 LIT days in 2026 had an Asia-slot trade the same trading day: too thin to say anything. Level age, level touches, reclaim speed, level reuse, cut time by... |\n| Closest to a lead | **Size x1.25 on a level touched few times** (bottom third): LIT +$2,988 / +$1,040 (2021-25 +$1,220), eval path +$5,813 / +$66, funded flat; one more 2-week fail in Jan-Jun (+0.8 points), shuffle p... |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/2026-11-04_lit_ideas_round2.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Rejected ideas]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, second-strategies]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-04_second_strategies_night.md\"]\n---\n\n# Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K\n\n> no rule change for ATLAS or BLITZ (0 of 72 versions holds in both 2026 halves with the eval-speed and 2-week-fail tests); BLITZ is on a plateau and can go live on its own 25K as built; the best account set on MFFU is LIT 50K + ATLAS 25K + BLITZ 25K (3 funded accounts is the MFFU cap); a 4th or 6th MFFU account adds fees, not money; the Plutus app numbers (built 2026-10-19) are stale in 5 places\n\n**Full title:** Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K accounts (72 new versions, none beats today), how they correlate with LIT, and what 2 / 3 / 4 / 6 accounts...\n\n## Summary\nJob 27. Part 1 - ATLAS (EMA + MACD + S/R) and BLITZ (noise-area scalp) re-tested for the 25K accounts with 43 BLITZ and 29 ATLAS versions (stop multiple, entry window, daily loss cap, trades a day, hold time, breakeven, trail, day filter, partials, fixed targets, time-of-day / weekday / LIT-result / LIT-direction size, member weights, risk caps, member subsets, the LIT-over-ATLAS-over-BLITZ rule). Part 2 - the same 2026 sessions drive every account (so a bad week hits all of them) through the MFFU Builder rules (eval, paperwork, funded, buffer, payouts at 80%, 50% best-day rule, funded caps) for 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 6 accounts. Part 3 - the Plutus plans.json is checked against the current numbers.\n\n## The answer in one table\n| What | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| BLITZ on its own 25K tomorrow | **Go as built.** 43 versions of the rule: none is better in both halves. Today (account C, never opposite LIT / ATLAS): Jan-Jun 217 trades (8.5 a week), Jul-Oct 106 (6.8 a week); eval 2-week pass /... |\n| Stops on the 25K | BLITZ stop = 1.5 x ATR, median **61 pts = $125 risk per micro**. Eval size (40% of the $1,000 room = $400) is **3 micros** (1 to 5). At the funded $150 cap, **32% of trades already risk more than... |\n| Anything better for BLITZ? | Best leads are neutral: half off at 1.5R (Jan-Jun -$117, Jul-Oct +$176 a month of the $500 view, slot unchanged); hold 45-60 min (Jul-Oct +$776 / +$10, Jan-Jun -$359 / -$1,079). Tighter stops... |\n| Anything better for ATLAS? | **No.** 29 versions: members (all three is best in Jan-Jun), weights +-25%, risk caps $300-600, daily loss $300 / $450, time-of-day, weekday, size by LIT's result or direction. Nothing is better in... |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/2026-11-04_second_strategies_night.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Second strategies]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Research/Writeups/The money plan research.md","text":"---\ntype: research\ntags: [research, money]\nsource: [\"research/writeups/2026-11-03-money-plan.md\"]\n---\n\n# The money plan research\n\n> adopt-candidate (owner decides; needs MFFU's written OK on copying between your own accounts) - a Zlarior copy on the third MFFU slot (the free Builder 25K) pays $334 / $269 / -$7 a month (fleet preset, today's code) or $375 / $331 / -$15 (the 50K's room 0.8, needs a small fleet option) vs BLITZ on that 25K $286 / $52 / -$39 (H1 / H2 / 2021-25)\n\n**Full title:** The money plan on today's settings (2026-11-03 night) - put Zlarior on the free MFFU 25K instead of BLITZ (+$217-279 a month H2), then add LIT 50K copies at...\n\n## Summary\nOwner 2026-11-02 \"overall the amount of money we will make\". MFFU lets one person hold 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25Ks. Today - Zlarior (LIT) on the 50K, ATLAS on one 25K, the other 25K (BLITZ's) off. Every slot was priced the same way - 6 months of eval -> 2 sessions of paperwork -> funded -> MFFU payouts -> the next eval, net of every eval, 1,000 Monte Carlo paths of the real sessions, live eval and funded sizing, $1.90 a micro. Per month (H1 Jan-Jun 2026 / H2 Jul-Oct 2026 / 2021-25 reference) - LIT 50K $823 / $798 / -$7; ATLAS 25K $568 / $382 / -$2; LIT 25K $375 / $331 / -$15 (room 0.8) or $334 / $269 / -$7 (the fleet's 25K preset, 50% room); BLITZ 25K $286 / $52 / -$39. So the free 25K earns most as a Zlarior copy - better than BLITZ in all three spans. The three bots' daily results are uncorrelated (LIT-ATLAS -0.01 / -0.03 / +0.11), while Zlarior copies move together...\n\n## The answer in one table\n| Account slot (6 months, net of every eval) | $ a month H1 / H2 / 2021-25 | 2-week pass / fail H1 / H2 / 2021-25 | evals bought per 6 months (H2) | $ to you per eval bought (H2, 6 months) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Zlarior (LIT) MFFU 50K, live | **$823 / $798 / -$7** | 52.9/17.8, 58.6/20.9, 20.7/24.9 | 1.67 | ~$2,870 |\n| ATLAS MFFU 25K, live (atx_funded_day.json \"today\") | $568 / $382 / -$2 | 65.5/0.8 (H1) | 1.44 | ~$1,590 |\n| **Zlarior MFFU 25K, room 0.8 (the 50K's rule at half scale)** | **$375 / $331 / -$15** | 55.0/10.6, 57.8/17.3, 20.0/23.2 | 1.57 | ~$1,270 |\n| Zlarior MFFU 25K, the fleet's 25K preset (50% room) | $334 / $269 / -$7 | 53.0/0.9, 50.6/0.8, 15.1/1.3 | 1.14 | ~$1,420 |\n\nSource: `research/writeups/2026-11-03-money-plan.md`.\n\nRelated: [[Research hub]], [[What we learned]], [[Money hub]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Research hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Templates/Daily note.md","text":"---\ndate: \"{{date}}\"\ntags: [daily]\n---\n\n# {{date}}\n\n## Before the session\nRoom to the floor: \nDays since the last trade: \nBot on and its feed fresh? yes / no\nThe plan for today: \n\n## During\nTrade taken? If yes, make a Trade journal entry and link it here.\n\n## After\nResult in dollars: \nRules followed? yes / no\nOne line to remember: \n\n## Tasks\nWrite tasks as `- [ ] text` (a dash, a space, an empty box, a space, the text). Jarvis reads them.\n\nRoutine: [[Daily routine]] | Checklists: [[Trading checklists]] | [[Trade journal]]\n"},{"path":"Templates/Idea note.md","text":"---\ndate: \"{{date}}\"\ntags: [idea]\nstatus: new\n---\n\n# Idea {{title}}\n\n## The idea in one line\n\n## Why it might work\n\n## First check the graveyard\nIs it already in [[Rejected ideas]] or [[What we learned]]? Do not redo a dead end without a new angle.\n\n## How to test it\nPick on Jan-Jun 2026, check on Jul-Oct 2026, 2021-25 as a reference. It must stay at 2 to 4 trades a week. Show net dollars, dollars a month, profit factor and max drawdown.\n\nBack to [[Research hub]].\n"},{"path":"Templates/Kalshi bet review.md","text":"---\ndate: \"{{date}}\"\ntags: [kalshi, review]\nmarket: \nside: \nprice_cents: \nsize_dollars: \nresult: \n---\n\n# Kalshi bet {{date}}\n\n## The bet\nMarket, side, price in cents, size in dollars: \n\n## Why\nWhat edge did I think I had? Was it one of the pockets in [[Kalshi bot research]]?\n\n## Result\nWon or lost, dollars, the fee: \n\n## Lesson\nOne sentence: \n\nBack to [[Kalshi bot research]].\n"},{"path":"Templates/Templates.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [templates, moc]\n---\n\n# Templates\n\n> Five templates. In Obsidian: Settings > Core plugins > Templates, set the template folder to Templates, then insert one with the command palette.\n\n- [[Daily note]]\n- [[Trade journal entry]]\n- [[Weekly review]]\n- [[Kalshi bet review]]\n- [[Idea note]]\n\nBack to [[00 Home]].\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Templates/Trade journal entry.md","text":"---\ndate: \"{{date}}\"\ntags: [journal, trade]\nsetup: \nsize: \nplan_followed: \nemotion: \nlesson: \n---\n\n# Trade {{date}} {{time}}\n\n## Setup\nWhich level was swept? Was there an inducement, a displacement, the 5m shift, the 1m confirmation? See [[Zlarior LIT strategy]].\n\n## Size\nMicros: . Stop in points: . Risk in dollars: . Size follows the stop: [[Risk rules]].\n\n## Plan followed?\nyes / no. If no, which rule: \n\n## Result\nDollars: . R: . Net after costs: \n\n## Emotion\nBefore: . During: . After: \n\n## Lesson\nOne sentence: \n\nBack to [[Trade journal]].\n"},{"path":"Templates/Weekly review.md","text":"---\ndate: \"{{date}}\"\ntags: [weekly, review]\n---\n\n# Week of {{date}}\n\n## The numbers\nTrades: . Wins: . Dollars: . Days with no trade: \n\n## Rules\nDid I follow the plan on every trade? Where not, and why: \n\n## What worked\n\n## What to change\nOnly change a rule with two-halves proof: [[Rejected ideas]] first.\n\n## Next week\nAnything on the calendar (news, a payout, an inactivity clock)? See [[Playbooks]].\n\nBack to [[Trade journal]] | [[Goals]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/After the session checklist.md","text":"---\ntype: checklist\ntags: [trading, checklist]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/library.json\"]\n---\n\n# After the session checklist\n\n> After the session: 3 rules from the daily routine. Copy the ones you want into today's daily note as tasks.\n\n1. Everything is flat by 15:55. Look at the result. ([[Daily routine]])\n2. Write one line in your notes (say \"note: ...\" to Jarvis). ([[Daily routine]])\n3. After a loss, do the loss playbook: no revenge trade, no change to the rules. ([[When I have a loss]])\n\nBack to [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading checklists]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/Before the session checklist.md","text":"---\ntype: checklist\ntags: [trading, checklist]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/library.json\"]\n---\n\n# Before the session checklist\n\n> Before the session: 4 rules from the daily routine. Copy the ones you want into today's daily note as tasks.\n\n1. Open Jarvis and read the first item on its list. ([[Daily routine]])\n2. Check the bot is on and its feed is not stale. ([[Zenith - the phone dashboard]])\n3. Check the days since the last trade (the account must trade every 7 days). ([[The 7-day rule]])\n4. Check the room left to the drawdown floor ($48,000). ([[The drawdown floor]])\n\nBack to [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading checklists]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/During the session checklist.md","text":"---\ntype: checklist\ntags: [trading, checklist]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/library.json\"]\n---\n\n# During the session checklist\n\n> During the session: 4 rules from the daily routine. Copy the ones you want into today's daily note as tasks.\n\n1. Let the bot trade. New entries start only from 02:00 to 15:30 New York time. ([[Daily routine]])\n2. Change nothing while a trade is open. The stop sits 1 tick beyond the sweep extreme and never moves further away. ([[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]])\n3. At most 1 trade a day. No long and short at the same time. ([[No long and short at once]])\n4. Do not trade by hand unless Ares says GO. ([[Ares - the war room]])\n\nBack to [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading checklists]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/Money checklist.md","text":"---\ntype: checklist\ntags: [trading, checklist]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/library.json\"]\n---\n\n# Money checklist\n\n> Money: 4 rules from the daily routine. Copy the ones you want into today's daily note as tasks.\n\n1. Daily loss limit: $1,000. Hit it and the day is over. ([[The daily loss limit]])\n2. Never more than 40 micros at once. ([[How many contracts]])\n3. Profit target for the eval: $3,000. ([[The profit target]])\n4. Ask for a payout only when the buffer is there (see the payout entry). ([[Payouts]])\n\nBack to [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading checklists]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/Never checklist.md","text":"---\ntype: checklist\ntags: [trading, checklist]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/athena/library.json\"]\n---\n\n# Never checklist\n\n> Never: 3 rules from the daily routine. Copy the ones you want into today's daily note as tasks.\n\n1. Never arm live trading for the owner. Paper and Sim only; the owner goes live himself. ([[When I want to go live]])\n2. Never put a password, key or card number in a note or in chat. ([[When I want to go live]])\n3. Never add a new rule from one good week. A new idea must hold in 2026 and in 2021-25. ([[When I want to try a new idea]])\n\nBack to [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading checklists]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Checklists/Trading checklists.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [trading, checklist, moc]\n---\n\n# Trading checklists\n\n> The daily routine as five short lists.\n\n- [[Before the session checklist]]\n- [[During the session checklist]]\n- [[After the session checklist]]\n- [[Money checklist]]\n- [[Never checklist]]\n\nThe routine itself: [[Daily routine]]. The templates: [[Templates]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [trading, glossary, moc]\n---\n\n# Glossary\n\n> Every term in plain words, one note per term. The links between them show how the ideas fit.\n\n- [[1m and 5m candles]] - One bar of price for one minute or five minutes. The bot finds setups on 5m and enters on 1m.\n- [[ATR]] - Average true range: how far a candle usually moves lately. The bot measures things in ATRs so they fit the day.\n- [[Breakeven]] - Moving the stop to your entry (plus costs) so the trade cannot lose.\n- [[Buffer]] - Extra profit you must hold on a funded account before the first payout. $2,100 on the 50K.\n- [[CISD]] - Change in state of delivery: price closes back above the start of the last run of falling candles, showing sellers lost control.\n- [[Commission]] - The fee per trade. About $1.9 round trip per micro.\n- [[Consistency rule]] - On funded accounts your best day must be under 50% of the cycle's profit, so one lucky day cannot carry a payout.\n- [[Daily loss limit]] - How much you may lose in one day. Soft: you are paused, not failed.\n- [[Displacement]] - One strong candle that shows real force. The bot needs a body of at least 0.8 ATR.\n- [[Drawdown]] - How far your account falls from its best point. The max drawdown is the worst fall in a test.\n- [[Drawdown floor]] - The balance at which the account fails. $48,000 at the start of a 50K eval.\n- [[EOD trailing drawdown]] - A floor that follows your best end-of-day balance up and never goes down. It locks at start + $100.\n- [[Eval]] - The test account you pay for. Hit +$3,000 without touching the floor and you pass.\n- [[Funded account]] - The account you get after passing. You trade it (simulated) and request payouts.\n- [[FVG]] - Fair value gap: a three-candle jump that leaves a hole where little trading happened.\n- [[IFVG]] - Inverse fair value gap: a gap that price filled, then flipped to the other side. A signal older models use; this bot uses the CISD instead.\n- [[Inducement (the trap)]] - A small swing that lures traders in just before the real move, so they get stopped out.\n- [[Limit order]] - An order to buy at a price or better, waiting for price to come to you.\n- [[Liquidity]] - Obvious highs and lows where lots of traders have parked stops and orders. Price is pulled toward them.\n- [[Micro]] - A micro contract: MNQ. Ten micros equal one mini (NQ).\n- [[MNQ and NQ]] - NQ is the Nasdaq-100 future ($20 a point). MNQ is the micro, a tenth of it ($2 a point).\n- [[NinjaTrader]] - The trading platform the PC bot sends orders to (Sim by default).\n- [[Paper trading]] - Practice trading with fake money, so a bug cannot cost a cent.\n- [[Payout]] - A withdrawal of profit from a funded account. You keep 80%.\n- [[PDH - PDL]] - Previous day high and low: yesterday's top and bottom, a classic level traders watch.\n- [[PF (profit factor)]] - Money won divided by money lost. Above 1 makes money. 2.91 means $2.91 won per $1 lost.\n- [[Point]] - One full NQ point (4 ticks). One MNQ is worth $2 per point.\n- [[R]] - Your risk on a trade. If you risk $300 and win $600, that is 2R. Losing the stop is -1R.\n- [[Scale-in]] - Adding to a trade that is already working. Built into the bot but switched OFF for now.\n- [[Session]] - A part of the day: Asia (evening), London (early morning), New York (morning). All times here are New York time.\n- [[Slippage]] - Getting a slightly worse price than you wanted. The tests include it.\n- [[Stop]] - The order that closes a trade at a loss if price goes against you. It defines your risk.\n- [[Sweep]] - Price runs through a level of liquidity, takes the orders there, then often turns around.\n- [[Tick]] - The smallest NQ move: 0.25 points. One MNQ tick is worth $0.50.\n- [[Trades a week]] - How often the bot trades. Aim: 2 to 4 a week.\n- [[VIX band]] - A fair guess of one day's move from the VIX fear index. The bot's target is the 18:00 open plus or minus one such move.\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/1m and 5m candles.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# 1m and 5m candles\n\n> One bar of price for one minute or five minutes. The bot finds setups on 5m and enters on 1m.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/ATR.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# ATR\n\n> Average true range: how far a candle usually moves lately. The bot measures things in ATRs so they fit the day.\n\n## Used in\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[Displacement]]\n- [[Momentum scalps for a third account]]\n- [[STOP-HUNT family - retail stop pools, the hunt, a resweep or inducement]]\n- [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]]\n- [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]]\n- [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]]\n- [[Sweep]]\n- [[Win rate and more trades]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Breakeven.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Breakeven\n\n> Moving the stop to your entry (plus costs) so the trade cannot lose.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Fancier exits]]\n- [[HQ LIT - can LIT win more often without losing its payoff Exits, A+]]\n- [[LIT experiments - sizing like a prop trader, a New York session, other]]\n- [[LIT funded accounts on the new live LIT (tcheck + scale-in + BE+1.25)]]\n- [[LIT tight stops and bigger size]]\n- [[Managing the trade]]\n- [[Parameter robustness]]\n- [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n- [[The LIT extras (none adopted)]]\n- [[Trade construction]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Buffer.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Buffer\n\n> Extra profit you must hold on a funded account before the first payout. $2,100 on the 50K.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Payouts]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/CISD.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# CISD\n\n> Change in state of delivery: price closes back above the start of the last run of falling candles, showing sellers lost control.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Cutting losses to pass more MFFU evals]]\n- [[IFVG]]\n- [[Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose]]\n- [[LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit]]\n- [[LIT final - one LIT strategy built from the four 2026 studies]]\n- [[LIT on the 1-minute chart]]\n- [[LIT untested entry ideas]]\n- [[Momentum scalps for a third account]]\n- [[Second strategies on LIT's account]]\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Commission.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Commission\n\n> The fee per trade. About $1.9 round trip per micro.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Consistency rule.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Consistency rule\n\n> On funded accounts your best day must be under 50% of the cycle's profit, so one lucky day cannot carry a payout.\n\n## Used in\n- [[MFFU Builder 25K next to the 50K]]\n- [[Payouts]]\n- [[The funded path - from a passed eval to money in the bank]]\n- [[The profit target]]\n- [[When I want a payout]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Daily loss limit.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Daily loss limit\n\n> How much you may lose in one day. Soft: you are paused, not failed.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Displacement.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Displacement\n\n> One strong candle that shows real force. The bot needs a body of at least 0.8 [[ATR]].\n\n## Used in\n- [[LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head]]\n- [[LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit]]\n- [[LIT on the 1-minute chart]]\n- [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Drawdown floor.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Drawdown floor\n\n> The balance at which the account fails. $48,000 at the start of a 50K eval.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Daily routine]]\n- [[EOD trailing drawdown]]\n- [[When I have a loss]]\n- [[Zenith - the phone dashboard]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Drawdown.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Drawdown\n\n> How far your account falls from its best point. The max drawdown is the worst fall in a test.\n\n## Used in\n- [[EOD trailing drawdown]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/EOD trailing drawdown.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# EOD trailing drawdown\n\n> A floor that follows your best end-of-day balance up and never goes down. It locks at start + $100.\n\nSee [[Drawdown|drawdown]] and [[Drawdown floor|drawdown floor]].\n\n## Used in\n- [[The drawdown floor]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Eval.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Eval\n\n> The test account you pay for. Hit +$3,000 without touching the floor and you pass.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/FVG.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# FVG\n\n> Fair value gap: a three-candle jump that leaves a hole where little trading happened.\n\n## Used in\n- [[IFVG]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Funded account.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Funded account\n\n> The account you get after passing. You trade it (simulated) and request payouts.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/IFVG.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# IFVG\n\n> Inverse fair value gap: a gap that price filled, then flipped to the other side. A signal older models use; this bot uses the [[CISD]] instead.\n\nSee also [[FVG]].\n\n## Used in\n- [[Famous models as taught]]\n- [[Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Inducement (the trap).md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Inducement (the trap)\n\n> A small swing that lures traders in just before the real move, so they get stopped out.\n\n## Used in\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head]]\n- [[LIT ablation - what each rule earns, what can go, which knobs sit]]\n- [[LIT losses - where and why LIT loses in 2026, and the rules worth adding]]\n- [[LIT on the 1-minute chart]]\n- [[LIT trade selection]]\n- [[LIT untested entry ideas]]\n- [[STOP-HUNT family - retail stop pools, the hunt, a resweep or inducement]]\n- [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Limit order.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Limit order\n\n> An order to buy at a price or better, waiting for price to come to you.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 5 - the entry]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Liquidity.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Liquidity\n\n> Obvious highs and lows where lots of traders have parked stops and orders. Price is pulled toward them.\n\n## Used in\n- [[The bot in one minute]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/MNQ and NQ.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# MNQ and NQ\n\n> NQ is the Nasdaq-100 future ($20 a point). MNQ is the micro, a tenth of it ($2 a point).\n\n## Used in\n- [[The bot in one minute]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Micro.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Micro\n\n> A micro contract: MNQ. Ten micros equal one mini (NQ).\n\n## Used in\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[BLITZ (the noise-area scalp) on MFFU]]\n- [[Eval size - one winner passes]]\n- [[Funded size]]\n- [[HQ LIT - can LIT win more often without losing its payoff Exits, A+]]\n- [[How many contracts]]\n- [[LIT funded sizing - how big to trade once the MFFU eval is passed]]\n- [[LIT two-week eval - how to pass the MFFU 50K in 10 sessions or less]]\n- [[MFFU Builder 25K next to the 50K]]\n- [[Momentum scalps for a third account]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/NinjaTrader.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# NinjaTrader\n\n> The trading platform the PC bot sends orders to (Sim by default).\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/PDH - PDL.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# PDH - PDL\n\n> Previous day high and low: yesterday's top and bottom, a classic level traders watch.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 1 - mark the levels]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/PF (profit factor).md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# PF (profit factor)\n\n> Money won divided by money lost. Above 1 makes money. 2.91 means $2.91 won per $1 lost.\n\n## Used in\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[A strategy uncorrelated with LIT]]\n- [[BLITZ (the noise-area scalp) on MFFU]]\n- [[Bias, filters and timing]]\n- [[Cross-market and regime context]]\n- [[Famous models as taught]]\n- [[How good is it The headline]]\n- [[Intraday anatomy - when, how and where the bot's trades make or lose]]\n- [[LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head]]\n- [[LIT final - one LIT strategy built from the four 2026 studies]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Paper trading.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Paper trading\n\n> Practice trading with fake money, so a bug cannot cost a cent.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Payout.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Payout\n\n> A withdrawal of profit from a funded account. You keep 80%.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Point.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Point\n\n> One full NQ point (4 ticks). One MNQ is worth $2 per point.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/R.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# R\n\n> Your risk on a trade. If you risk $300 and win $600, that is 2R. Losing the stop is -1R.\n\nThe bot's target must be at least 1.5R and it banks at 8R.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Money, not only R]]\n- [[Odeon - trade replays]]\n- [[Step 7 - the target]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Scale-in.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Scale-in\n\n> Adding to a trade that is already working. Built into the bot but switched OFF for now.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Session.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Session\n\n> A part of the day: Asia (evening), London (early morning), New York (morning). All times here are New York time.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 1 - mark the levels]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Slippage.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Slippage\n\n> Getting a slightly worse price than you wanted. The tests include it.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Stop.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Stop\n\n> The order that closes a trade at a loss if price goes against you. It defines your risk.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 6 - the stop]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Sweep.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Sweep\n\n> Price runs through a level of liquidity, takes the orders there, then often turns around.\n\nThe bot needs at least half an [[ATR]] past the level.\n\n## Used in\n- [[A second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[LIT (Liquidity Inducement Theorem) built as its own strategy, head]]\n- [[LIT losses - where and why LIT loses in 2026, and the rules worth adding]]\n- [[LIT on the 1-minute chart]]\n- [[LIT tight stops and bigger size]]\n- [[LIT untested entry ideas]]\n- [[Second strategies on LIT's account]]\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT (learned)]]\n- [[Step 1 - mark the levels]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Tick.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Tick\n\n> The smallest NQ move: 0.25 points. One MNQ tick is worth $0.50.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 6 - the stop]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/Trades a week.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Trades a week\n\n> How often the bot trades. Aim: 2 to 4 a week.\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Glossary/VIX band.md","text":"---\ntype: term\ntags: [trading, words]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# VIX band\n\n> A fair guess of one day's move from the VIX fear index. The bot's target is the 18:00 open plus or minus one such move.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Second strategy next to LIT]]\n- [[Step 7 - the target]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Glossary]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules that matter.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [trading, mffu, moc]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# MFFU rules that matter\n\n> The prop firm's rules that decide whether the account lives. Source: `research/lit/mffu_rules.json` (web research 2026-10-18; re-check before the first live payout).\n\n## Builder 50K (the eval account)\n| Rule | Value | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Profit target (eval) | $3,000 | confirmed |\n| Max loss | $2,000, end-of-day trailing (the [[The drawdown floor]]), locks at start + $100 | confirmed |\n| Daily loss limit | $1,000, soft: trading pauses for that session | confirmed |\n| Largest size | 40 micros or 4 minis | confirmed |\n| Flat by | 16:10 ET; no overnight or weekend positions | confirmed |\n| Inactivity | no trade for 7 calendar days can close the account | confirmed |\n| Hedging | not allowed (no long and short at once, also across accounts) | confirmed |\n| Bots | allowed, with your own parameters; no high-frequency tricks, no exploiting simulated fills | confirmed |\n| Price | list $153; the owner paid $91.80; one-time, $0 activation | confirmed |\n\n## When funded (sim funded)\n| Rule | Value | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buffer | $2,100 above the start before the first payout | confirmed |\n| First payout | at least $500 net profit above the buffer (balance >= $2,600) and 48 hours after the first trade | confirmed |\n| Payout size | $500 to $2,000; you keep 80% | confirmed |\n| Best-day rule | no single day may be more than 50% of the profit | confirmed |\n| Cycle and count | one payout every 2 winning days, up to 5 sim payouts | confirmed |\n\n## Builder 25K (ATLAS and BLITZ)\n| Rule | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Profit target | $1,500 |\n| Max loss | $1,000 (end-of-day trailing) |\n| Daily loss limit | $600 |\n| Largest size | 20 micros |\n| Buffer and payouts | buffer $1,100, payouts $250 to $1,000 |\n| Price | usually $63; $43 on 2026-10-26 |\n\n## Caps\nMFFU lets one person hold 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25K: 3 accounts is the cap. Extra accounts must be another firm.\n\n## The notes behind these rules\n- [[The drawdown floor]] - Your account fails if your balance, with any open trade, touches the floor. On the 50K the floor starts at $48,000.\n- [[The daily loss limit]] - On the 50K the daily loss limit is $1,000. It is soft: hit it and you are paused for the session, but the account stays open.\n- [[The profit target]] - The 50K eval passes at +$3,000. There is no consistency rule rule in the eval. The 25K needs +$1,500.\n- [[How many contracts]] - The 50K allows 40 micros or 4 minis, counted together: 10 micros = 1 mini. The 25K allows 20 micros.\n- [[Flat by 16 -10]] - Trading is intraday only. You must be flat by 16:10 New York time and no weekend holds. The bot is flat by 15:55 to be safe.\n- [[No long and short at once]] - You may not hold a long and a short on the same market at the same time, also across your accounts.\n- [[Bots are allowed]] - Bots are allowed with your own settings and you watching. No high-frequency trading and no exploiting simulated fills.\n- [[News]] - News is allowed in the eval and on a sim-funded account. On a live account MFFU advises against trading it.\n- [[The 7-day rule]] - Trade at least once every 7 days or the account can be closed.\n- [[Payouts]] - On a funded account the first payout opens once you are $500 above the $2,100 buffer, so a balance of $52,600. Payouts run $500 to $2,000, and 80% is...\n- [[What the evals cost you]] - You paid $91.80 for the Builder 50K and $63 for the Builder 25K. One-time payment, no activation fee, no monthly renewal.\n\nSee also [[Risk rules]], [[Playbooks]] and [[Glossary]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/Bots are allowed.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# Bots are allowed\n\n> Bots are allowed with your own settings and you watching. No high-frequency trading and no exploiting simulated fills.\n\nMFFU requires you to be present for live trading.\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/Flat by 16 -10.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# Flat by 16 -10\n\n> Trading is intraday only. You must be flat by 16:10 New York time and no weekend holds. The bot is flat by 15:55 to be safe.\n\nThe platform closes you at 16:10. Never force orders after it.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** MFFU needs you flat by what time?\n**A:** 16:10 New York time\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/How many contracts.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# How many contracts\n\n> The 50K allows 40 micros or 4 minis, counted together: 10 micros = 1 mini. The 25K allows 20 micros.\n\nOver the limit, the order is rejected.\n\nA [[Micro|micro]] is the small contract.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Money checklist]]\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/News.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# News\n\n> News is allowed in the eval and on a sim-funded account. On a live account MFFU advises against trading it.\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/No long and short at once.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# No long and short at once\n\n> You may not hold a long and a short on the same market at the same time, also across your accounts.\n\nThat is why one strategy always has priority when two want opposite sides.\n\n## Used in\n- [[During the session checklist]]\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/Payouts.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# Payouts\n\n> On a funded account the first payout opens once you are $500 above the $2,100 [[Buffer|buffer]], so a balance of $52,600. Payouts run $500 to $2,000, and 80% is yours.\n\nYour best day must stay under 50% of the cycle's profit ([[Consistency rule|consistency rule]]).\n\nAfter 5 sim payouts the account moves to live.\n\nCheck MFFU's own pages before asking for a payout. The rules were last checked on 2026-10-18.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** What share of each payout is yours?\n**A:** 80%\n\n## Used in\n- [[Money checklist]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/The 7-day rule.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# The 7-day rule\n\n> Trade at least once every 7 days or the account can be closed.\n\nThe bot normally trades 2 to 4 times a week, so this only matters in a quiet or holiday week.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** How often must you trade so the account is not closed?\n**A:** At least once every 7 days\n\n## Used in\n- [[Before the session checklist]]\n- [[Daily routine]]\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/The daily loss limit.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu, words]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# The daily loss limit\n\n> On the 50K the daily loss limit is $1,000. It is soft: hit it and you are paused for the session, but the account stays open.\n\nThe bot keeps every full stop-out inside this number.\n\nThe Builder 25K limit is $600.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Money checklist]]\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/The drawdown floor.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu, words]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# The drawdown floor\n\n> Your account fails if your balance, with any open trade, touches the floor. On the 50K the floor starts at $48,000.\n\nThe floor only moves up, and only at the end of the day, from your best end-of-day balance (this is [[EOD trailing drawdown]]).\n\nIt stops at $50,100 (start + $100) and stays there.\n\nAn open loss counts the moment it happens, not at the close.\n\nThe room to the floor is what the bot sizes against.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** On the Builder 50K, what happens if your balance touches the floor?\n**A:** The account fails\n\n## Used in\n- [[Before the session checklist]]\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/The profit target.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# The profit target\n\n> The 50K eval passes at +$3,000. There is no [[Consistency rule|consistency rule]] rule in the eval. The 25K needs +$1,500.\n\nThere is no time limit counting down on the eval. The 7-day inactivity rule still applies.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Money checklist]]\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/MFFU rules/What the evals cost you.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, mffu, numbers]\nsource: [\"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# What the evals cost you\n\n> You paid $91.80 for the Builder 50K and $63 for the Builder 25K. One-time payment, no activation fee, no monthly renewal.\n\nList prices are higher. Always use your real price when comparing.\n\n---\nUp: [[MFFU rules that matter]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Risk rules.md","text":"---\ntype: rule\ntags: [trading, risk]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\", \"research/lit/mffu_rules.json\"]\n---\n\n# Risk rules\n\n> Size follows the stop. The stop sits where the idea is wrong, never a random distance. A wider stop means fewer micros, not skipping the trade.\n\n## The rules\n1. Stop = 1 tick beyond the sweep extreme (the protected high or low). See [[Step 6 - the stop]].\n2. Size follows the stop and the room left to the floor: [[Eval size - one winner passes]], [[Funded size]].\n3. One clear target (the VIX band), banked at 8R. No greed. See [[Step 7 - the target]].\n4. One trade a day at most: [[One trade a day]].\n5. Paper only until the owner arms live himself: [[Paper only, you go live]].\n6. A new rule must hold in 2026 AND in 2021-25 and keep 2 to 4 trades a week. See [[When I want to try a new idea]].\n\n## The account limits\n- [[The drawdown floor]] - Your account fails if your balance, with any open trade, touches the floor. On the 50K the floor starts at $48,000.\n- [[The daily loss limit]] - On the 50K the daily loss limit is $1,000. It is soft: hit it and you are paused for the session, but the account stays open.\n- [[How many contracts]] - The 50K allows 40 micros or 4 minis, counted together: 10 micros = 1 mini. The 25K allows 20 micros.\n- [[Flat by 16 -10]] - Trading is intraday only. You must be flat by 16:10 New York time and no weekend holds. The bot is flat by 15:55 to be safe.\n- [[No long and short at once]] - You may not hold a long and a short on the same market at the same time, also across your accounts.\n- [[The 7-day rule]] - Trade at least once every 7 days or the account can be closed.\n\n## After a bad day\n- [[When I have a loss]] - A stop-out is a normal cost of the system, not a mistake. Do nothing special: no revenge trade, no bigger size.\n- [[When two losses in a row]] - A short losing run is expected. The system's worst runs in testing were still inside the limits. Keep the size the bot gives you.\n- [[When I hit the daily loss limit]] - You are paused for the session, not failed. Stop for the day.\n\nMore: [[MFFU rules that matter]], [[Trading checklists]], [[Never checklist]].\n\n## Used in\n- [[Me]]\n- [[Trade journal entry]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Second strategies.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy, atlas, blitz]\nsource: [\"tools/web_public/plutus/plans.json\", \"research/writeups/atlas.md\", \"research/writeups/blitz-25k.md\"]\n---\n\n# Second strategies\n\n> Zlarior (LIT) is the main bot on the 50K. ATLAS and BLITZ are two more strategies for the 25K accounts. All are paper first; the owner arms live himself.\n\n## ATLAS (EMA + MACD + support/resistance, account B)\n- Runs on a Builder 25K (eval price paid $43). Eval within 2 weeks: 65.5% pass in Jan-Jun 2026, 43.5% in Jul-Oct; 2-week fails 0% / 0%.\n- Funded: about $582 a month (Jan-Jun) and $517 (Jul-Oct); net per month counting the purchase: $623 / $519.\n- Latest research verdict: adopt-candidate (unchanged rules; built, OFF; paper / Sim first)\n\n## BLITZ (a noise-area scalp, account C)\n- Its own Builder 25K (price $63). Eval within 2 weeks: 49.6% pass / 26.9% fail in Jan-Jun; 36.2% / 21.7% in Jul-Oct.\n- Net per month counting the purchase: $289 (Jan-Jun) and $52 (Jul-Oct): the thinnest of the three.\n- Latest research verdict: no adopt-candidate - no BLITZ rule change holds in 2026 H1, H2 and 2021-25 (0 of 1,997 variants); verdict no-change (research/scalp/blitz_25k_pick.json). Only the 25K eval size for its own account changes (40% of the room, then 100% from session 5): about 72% of evals decided in 2 weeks instead of 40%, same money. The 25K slot still loses about $40 a month in 2021-25 with every setting. Paper only, nothing switched.\n\n## How the three fit together\n- Copies of one strategy take the same trades (MFFU forbids long and short at once across your accounts), so those accounts rise and fall together: the study measured a 0.85 correlation between two of them. LIT, ATLAS and BLITZ are different strategies: their daily correlation in 2026 is LIT x ATLAS +0.14, LIT x BLITZ -0.06, ATLAS x BLITZ 0.00.\n- MFFU lets one person hold 1 funded Builder 50K and 2 funded Builder 25K: 3 accounts is the cap. Extra accounts must be another firm.\n\nMore: [[Accounts plan]], [[Plutus numbers]], [[Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K]], [[ATLAS research - account B on the Builder 25K]], [[BLITZ research - its own Builder 25K]].\n\n## Used in\n- [[ATLAS research - account B on the Builder 25K]]\n- [[Accounts plan]]\n- [[BLITZ research - its own Builder 25K]]\n- [[Second strategies night - ATLAS and BLITZ on the 25K]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Eval size - one winner passes.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy, mffu]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\", \"research/writeups/uprisk.md\"]\n---\n\n# Eval size - one winner passes\n\n> In an eval the bot buys enough [[Micro|micro]] contracts that one winning trade to its target reaches the +$3,000 goal, but never risks more than 80% of the room to the floor.\n\nThe target is known at entry, so the bot can work out how many micros one winner needs.\n\nA full stop-out must stay inside 80% of the room and inside the $1,000 daily loss.\n\nThe cost: about 1 eval in 5 fails within 2 weeks. The gain: most evals are decided within 2 weeks.\n\nNear the target the size shrinks by itself.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Funded size.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy, mffu]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Funded size\n\n> On a funded account the bot risks 60% of the room to the floor, at most $1,500 a trade, and a winner is capped at $2,000.\n\nThe cap on a winner is one full payout request.\n\nWhen one [[Micro|micro]] could reach the floor, the bot skips.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/One trade a day.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# One trade a day\n\n> The bot takes one trade a day at most, and never two at once. Expect 2 to 4 trades a week.\n\nFewer than 2 a week is too slow for an eval. The rule is: never make a filter that pauses trading for weeks.\n\nA quiet day with no setup is normal. It is not a bug.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Paper only, you go live.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy, mffu]\nsource: [\"CLAUDE.md\"]\n---\n\n# Paper only, you go live\n\n> The bot trades paper (simulated) money. It never arms live trading for you. Going live on the eval is your own decision and MFFU needs you present.\n\nNo real order is placed unless you switch it on yourself.\n\nKeys and secrets never go in the repo or in chat.\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 1 - mark the levels.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 1 - mark the levels\n\n> Before anything happens, the bot lists the obvious highs and lows: session highs and lows, yesterday, last week, last month, opening gaps, equal highs or lows.\n\nAsia, London and New York [[Session|session]] highs and lows count, once their box has closed.\n\n[[PDH - PDL]] and PDL (yesterday), the week and the month high and low count.\n\nThe weekly and daily opening gaps count, and so do \"equal lows\": two swing lows at the same price, untouched in between.\n\nA level is marked before the [[Sweep|sweep]], never after. Marking it afterwards would be cheating the test.\n\nNext: [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Oracle - the live radar]]\n- [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 2 - wait for a sweep.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 2 - wait for a sweep\n\n> Price makes a new 2-hour low (or high) and runs through a marked level (a [[Sweep|sweep]]) by at least half an [[ATR]].\n\nA swept low sets up a long (buy). A swept high sets up a short (sell). Shorts are the mirror image of everything here.\n\nHalf an ATR is the filter that stops tiny pokes from counting.\n\nBefore: [[Step 1 - mark the levels]] | Next: [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 1 - mark the levels]]\n- [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 3 - the trap (inducement).md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 3 - the trap (inducement)\n\n> Just before the [[Sweep|sweep]] a small swing low sat a little above the level. The final push ran through it, so early buyers got stopped out: that is the [[Inducement (the trap)|inducement (the trap)]].\n\nThe small swing is at most 1 [[ATR]] above the level and at most 3 hours old.\n\nNothing may touch it until the last push into the low.\n\nBefore: [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]] | Next: [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[During the session checklist]]\n- [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]]\n- [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD).md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)\n\n> Price must close back above the start of the last fall. That close is the [[CISD]], proof the sellers lost control. One strong candle on the way ([[Displacement|displacement]]) is required.\n\nThe CISD level is the open of the first candle of the last run of down-closing 5-minute candles.\n\nOne candle body must be at least 0.8 [[ATR]] (that is the displacement).\n\nThe bot only looks for entries between 02:00 and 15:30 New York time.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** In the bot's rules, which hours can a new trade start?\n**A:** 02:00 to 15:30 New York time\n\nBefore: [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]] | Next: [[Step 5 - the entry]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]]\n- [[Step 5 - the entry]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 5 - the entry.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 5 - the entry\n\n> A buy [[Limit order|limit order]] sits at the CISD level for 30 minutes. After price taps it, the bot waits for a small 1-minute [[CISD]], then buys.\n\nOnce price taps the limit, the bot gives it up to 20 one-minute candles to confirm.\n\nThe setup is dropped if the [[Sweep|sweep]] low breaks, the target trades first, 30 minutes pass with no tap, or it is 15:55.\n\nThe bot never chases with a market order. A missed move stays missed.\n\nBefore: [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]] | Next: [[Step 6 - the stop]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]]\n- [[Step 6 - the stop]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 6 - the stop.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 6 - the stop\n\n> The [[Stop|stop]] goes 1 [[Tick|tick]] beyond the extreme of the [[Sweep|sweep]], never a fixed distance. Under 8 points or over 80 points means no trade.\n\nThe sweep extreme is the high or low made before the turn. If price goes back through it, the idea was wrong, so you are out.\n\nA wide stop means fewer [[Micro|micro]] contracts (less risk). A small stop allows more. Size follows the stop.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Where does the bot put its stop?\n**A:** 1 tick beyond the sweep extreme\n\nBefore: [[Step 5 - the entry]] | Next: [[Step 7 - the target]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n- [[Step 5 - the entry]]\n- [[Step 7 - the target]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 7 - the target.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 7 - the target\n\n> The target is the 18:00 open plus (long) or minus (short) one day's expected move from the [[VIX band]]. It must be at least 1.5R away, and profit is banked at 8R.\n\nOne move = yesterday's VIX close divided by 100, divided by the square root of 252, times the 18:00 open.\n\nCloser than 1.5[[R]] means no trade. Further than 8R means take the money at 8R. No greed.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** Where does the bot bank profit at most?\n**A:** At 8R\n\nBefore: [[Step 6 - the stop]] | Next: [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Risk rules]]\n- [[Step 6 - the stop]]\n- [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 8 - manage the trade.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 8 - manage the trade\n\n> At +1R the stop moves to [[Breakeven|breakeven]] (plus 1.25 points for costs). A trade that is not working 15 minutes after the fill is closed early. Everything is flat by 15:55.\n\nBreakeven: after +1R the stop goes to entry + 1.25 points, so a breakeven exit does not lose the costs.\n\n15-minute check: if the trade never reached +0.5R and is more than 0.3R under water, it is closed at the next 1-minute open.\n\nFlat by 15:55 New York time. MFFU needs you flat by 16:10, so there is a safety gap.\n\nAdding to a winner ([[Scale-in|scale-in]]) is built but OFF until a Sim test passes.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** When is everything flat?\n**A:** 15:55 New York time\n\nBefore: [[Step 7 - the target]] | Next: [[Step 9 - the size]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 7 - the target]]\n- [[Step 9 - the size]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/Step 9 - the size.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Step 9 - the size\n\n> Size follows the stop: about $800 of risk up to a 20-point stop, then less as the stop gets wider. Evals use a special size so one winner can pass.\n\n$800 risk up to a 20-point stop, then 800 x 20 / stop. A 40-point stop risks $400, an 80-point stop $200.\n\n[[Micro|micro]] count = risk / (stop x $2), rounded down, at most 40. Under 1 micro means no trade.\n\nAfter 2 stop-outs in a week the size is halved for the rest of that week (outside the eval sizing).\n\nBefore: [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n\n## Used in\n- [[Step 8 - manage the trade]]\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Strategy/The bot in one minute.md","text":"---\ntype: strategy\ntags: [trading, strategy, numbers]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# The bot in one minute\n\n> The bot waits for price to poke through an obvious high or low, trap the late traders, then turn. It buys (or sells) the turn with a stop right behind the poke.\n\nBig groups of traders park their stops and orders at obvious highs and lows. That pile of orders is [[Liquidity|liquidity]].\n\nPrice often runs through such a level (a [[Sweep|sweep]]), traps the traders who reacted, then reverses. The bot trades that reversal.\n\nIt is one fixed set of written rules, called LIT. It trades NQ futures as [[MNQ and NQ]], using [[Micro|micro]] contracts, one trade a day at most, paper only.\n\nEvery rule was picked on Jan to Jun 2026 and checked on Jul to Oct 2026.\n\n## Check yourself\n**Q:** What is the idea behind the bot?\n**A:** Trade the turn after price pokes through an obvious high or low and traps traders\n\n---\nUp: [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] | [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Trading hub.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [trading, moc]\n---\n\n# Trading hub\n\n> Everything about how the NQ bots trade and what the prop firm allows.\n\n- [[Zlarior LIT strategy]] - the main bot in nine steps\n- [[Second strategies]] - ATLAS and BLITZ\n- [[MFFU rules that matter]] - the firm's limits\n- [[Risk rules]] - size follows the stop\n- [[Trading checklists]] - before, during, after, money, never\n- [[Daily routine]] - the simple day\n- [[Glossary]] - every term\n- [[Playbooks]] - what to do when something happens\n- [[Rejected ideas]] - the graveyard, check it before a new idea\n- [[Trade journal]] - where each trade is written down\n\n---\nUp: [[00 Home]]\n"},{"path":"Trading/Zlarior LIT strategy.md","text":"---\ntype: moc\ntags: [trading, strategy, moc]\nsource: [\"STRATEGY.md\"]\n---\n\n# Zlarior LIT strategy\n\n> The bot waits for price to poke through an obvious high or low, trap the late traders, then turn. It buys (or sells) the turn with a stop right behind the poke.\n\nBig groups of traders park their stops and orders at obvious highs and lows. That pile of orders is [[Liquidity|liquidity]].\n\nPrice often runs through such a level (a [[Sweep|sweep]]), traps the traders who reacted, then reverses. The bot trades that reversal.\n\nIt is one fixed set of written rules, called LIT. It trades NQ futures as [[MNQ and NQ]], using [[Micro|micro]] contracts, one trade a day at most, paper only.\n\nEvery rule was picked on Jan to Jun 2026 and checked on Jul to Oct 2026.\n\n## The nine steps\n1. [[Step 1 - mark the levels]] - Before anything happens, the bot lists the obvious highs and lows: session highs and lows, yesterday, last week, last month, opening gaps...\n2. [[Step 2 - wait for a sweep]] - Price makes a new 2-hour low (or high) and runs through a marked level (a sweep) by at least half an ATR.\n3. [[Step 3 - the trap (inducement)]] - Just before the sweep a small swing low sat a little above the level. The final push ran through it, so early buyers got stopped out: that...\n4. [[Step 4 - the shift (5m CISD)]] - Price must close back above the start of the last fall. That close is the CISD, proof the sellers lost control. One strong candle on the...\n5. [[Step 5 - the entry]] - A buy limit order sits at the CISD level for 30 minutes. After price taps it, the bot waits for a small 1-minute CISD, then buys.\n6. [[Step 6 - the stop]] - The stop goes 1 tick beyond the extreme of the sweep, never a fixed distance. Under 8 points or over 80 points means no trade.\n7. [[Step 7 - the target]] - The target is the 18:00 open plus (long) or minus (short) one day's expected move from the VIX band. It must be at least 1.5R away, and...\n8. [[Step 8 - manage the trade]] - At +1R the stop moves to breakeven (plus 1.25 points for costs). A trade that is not working 15 minutes after the fill is closed early....\n9. [[Step 9 - the size]] - Size follows the stop: about $800 of risk up to a 20-point stop, then less as the stop gets wider. Evals use a special size so one winner...\n\n## Size, trades a day, paper\n- [[One trade a day]] - The bot takes one trade a day at most, and never two at once. Expect 2 to 4 trades a week.\n- [[Eval size - one winner passes]] - In an eval the bot buys enough micro contracts that one winning trade to its target reaches the +$3,000 goal, but never risks more than 80% of the...\n- [[Funded size]] - On a funded account the bot risks 60% of the room to the floor, at most $1,500 a trade, and a winner is capped at $2,000.\n- [[Paper only, you go live]] - The bot trades paper (simulated) money. It never arms live trading for you. Going live on the eval is your own decision and MFFU needs you present.\n\n## The numbers\n- [[How good is it The headline]] - 2026 so far: PF (profit factor) 4.19, +$43,820 on paper with costs in. The honest number to expect is the later half: PF 2.91, +$9,919 (about $2,670...\n- [[Money, not only R]] - Every result shows net dollars and dollars a month next to PF (profit factor) and R. R alone hides how much real money is at stake.\n- [[Eval speed - 2 weeks]] - An eval should be passed or failed within 2 weeks (10 sessions) and never take more than 3 weeks to pass.\n- [[Two halves rule]] - A rule change is only kept if it also works on the second half of the data it was not picked on.\n\n## Where to go next\n- The exact rulebook is `STRATEGY.md` in the repo (this note is the plain-words version).\n- What you can do with it today: [[Daily routine]] and [[Trading checklists]]. When something goes wrong: [[Playbooks]].\n- What was tried and dropped: [[Rejected ideas]]. Words you do not know: [[Glossary]]. The prop firm's limits: [[MFFU rules that matter]].\n- The second and third bots: [[Second strategies]].\n\n---\nUp: [[Trading hub]] | [[00 Home]]\n"}]};
